"""Original Security teaching packs. Each body is instructor prose."""

BODIES = {
    'WPA3': {
        'body': 'WPA3 uses simultaneous authentication of equals so a captured personal handshake is a poor prize for offline word-list grinding. WPA3 personal mode retires the older four-way exchange that handed an observer material worth testing in private later. WPA3 still depends on a passphrase for personal networks, yet that secret is bound into a fresh password-authenticated exchange each time a station associates. WPA3 gives each association its own session keys, so learning one session does not unlock traffic recorded on an earlier visit.\n\nWPA3 makes protected management frames mandatory, which blunts forged deauthentication and disassociation bursts from a casual spoofer. WPA3 enterprise mode can offer a 192-bit suite where sign-in is already certificate-backed and the site is ready for the heavier cipher suite. WPA3 transition mode keeps an older personal mode alive on the same network name, and that compromise reopens the weaker path on purpose. WPA3 belongs on the staff network as the only personal mode once clients can speak it, with legacy stations moved to a separate fenced segment.\n\nWPA3, together with opportunistic wireless encryption, is what six-gigahertz service expects, because older personal modes are not offered there. WPA3 shows up in a beacon robust-security element as an SAE capability and as management-frame protection that is required rather than optional. WPA3 does not by itself issue each person a distinct account, because per-person sign-in is an authentication-server decision sitting beside the air protection. WPA3 does not choose which programs a handset may install, because an allow-list lives on the device policy, not in the radio handshake.\n\nWPA3 code on controllers and access points has had its own defects, so a supported mode is unfinished until firmware follows the vendor fix stream. WPA3 configuration should drop older-only operation on the staff name after every maintained client can complete the new exchange. WPA3 guest service should use a different name and a different isolation rule, never a staff secret posted where visitors can read it. WPA3 passphrase quality still matters against online guessing at the access point, which is slower and noisier than a private grind of captured material.\n\nWPA3 controller logs should show which stations negotiated the new mode and which stations attempted a fallback. WPA3 survey notes should say whether management-frame protection is required on that name or merely advertised as capable. WPA3 client failures after a driver update are compatibility work, not a reason to put the staff name back into a mixed mode. WPA3 personal cipher choice is normally CCMP with AES, while the enterprise 192-bit profile steps the suite upward.\n\nWPA3 forward secrecy means frames captured last month stay unreadable even if the passphrase is learned this month. WPA3 on one staff name should not advertise both generations once the client fleet can be moved, because the older advertisement is a finding. WPA3 onboarding notes should tell people to forget a cached older profile so the station does not prefer it in the background. WPA3 enterprise failures are usually certificate or server problems, whereas a personal-mode failure is a passphrase problem or an SAE negotiation problem.\n\nWPA3 does not replace rogue-access-point detection, because a lookalike name can still be advertised by equipment you do not own. WPA3 is not client isolation, band steering, or transmit-power choice, and a ticket should not use those labels when the evidence is the handshake family. WPA3 tickets that say only that wireless feels weak are incomplete until the mode, the transition flag, and the network name are written down. WPA3 changes belong on a spare name first, so a bad controller image can be reversed before voice handsets are stranded.\n\nWPA3 designs leave the older temporal-key integrity protocol behind, and that protocol should not remain enabled beside staff traffic. WPA3 personal secrets shared by a whole shift are still an identity weakness even when the air protection itself is current. WPA3 reviews should list every name that still allows the older personal mode and which segment those stations can reach. WPA3 evidence can be a beacon captured without joining, which is enough to read the advertised protection suite.\n\nWPA3 station tables on the controller should show the pairwise cipher and whether management frames on that association are protected. WPA3 does nothing to remove a departed person when the secret is a shared passphrase, which is why staff wireless should not stop at personal mode. WPA3 standards rows should name the controller, the site, the network name, and the modes that row allows. WPA3 legacy printers can sit on a segment that cannot open sessions toward staff hosts, rather than pulling the staff name backward.',
        'r0': "WPA3 personal links run a password-authenticated key agreement called SAE instead of recycling one pre-shared master secret an observer can grind through from a capture. WPA3 derives fresh session keys per association, which is forward secrecy for frames on the air. WPA3 forces protected management frames, so spoofed deauthentication is no longer a cheap way to knock stations off. WPA3 enterprise mode may step up to a 192-bit profile when certificates and stronger ciphers are already the site standard. WPA3 transition mode is only a bridge, because the older personal handshake stays available beside the new one. WPA3 on six-gigahertz radios is the baseline the band actually allows. WPA3 proof is the beacon suite plus the station's negotiated cipher, not a guess that the name sounds current.",
        'r1': 'WPA3 is newer wifi protection. WPA3 hinders offline passphrase grinding. WPA3 still has a personal passphrase. WPA3 makes fresh keys per join. WPA3 requires protected management frames. WPA3 transition mode keeps an older path. WPA3 staff names should drop mixed mode. WPA3 legacy clients use a fenced segment. WPA3 is not directory login per person. WPA3 is not a handset app allow-list. WPA3 gear still needs current firmware. WPA3 beacons show the real suite. WPA3 proof is the negotiated suite.',
        'r2': "WPA3 resembles a concert wristband whose hologram is pressed again for every performance. WPA3 in that picture makes a photo of last night's band useless at tonight's gate. WPA3 transition mode is the manager who still honors last year's paper strip. WPA3 for the staff entrance should refuse the paper strip. WPA3 legacy visitors can walk a side garden that never opens onto the stage. WPA3 protected management frames are ushers who ignore a stranger yelling that someone was removed. WPA3 does not decide which instruments may come through the loading dock. WPA3 firmware is the press, and a damaged press prints weak holograms until the shop repairs it. WPA3 evidence is the hologram a door scanner actually reads, not a poster claiming the venue is modern.",
        'r4': 'WPA3: record the staff mode before any change. WPA3: require protected management frames on that name. WPA3: disable transition mode after clients are ready. WPA3: move older-only devices onto a fenced segment. WPA3: capture a beacon and read the suite. WPA3: treat per-person login as a separate choice. WPA3: patch controller firmware before closing the task. WPA3: write a backout if roaming handsets fail.',
        'exam': 'WPA3 items turn on a personal handshake that resists offline grinding and on management frames that must be protected. WPA3 fits when a beacon shows SAE and the staff name is not left mixed for convenience. WPA3 is the distractor when the stem describes one directory account per person. WPA3 is also the distractor when the stem is a ban on side-loaded handset programs.',
    },
    'RADIUS for wireless': {
        'body': "RADIUS for wireless lets the access point act as an authenticator while a central server decides whether a person's credentials are good. RADIUS for wireless sits inside 802.1X, with the station as the supplicant and an EAP method carrying the proof. RADIUS for wireless ties each association to a directory account, so disabling that account removes the ability to join at the next authentication. RADIUS for wireless replaces a passphrase known to a whole shift with a credential that can be revoked for one person.\n\nRADIUS for wireless accounting records who associated, from which called-station identifier, and when the session started and stopped. RADIUS for wireless uses a shared device secret between the network access server and the authentication server, and that secret is not the person's password. RADIUS for wireless commonly listens on UDP 1812 for access requests and UDP 1813 for accounting, with older deployments sometimes still on 1645 and 1646. RADIUS for wireless can return a VLAN, a filter name, or a session timeout inside the access-accept so the controller places the station in the right segment.\n\nRADIUS for wireless prefers EAP-TLS when both the station and the server present certificates, because a stolen password database does not by itself complete that method. RADIUS for wireless may use PEAP or EAP-TTLS when a password is tunneled inside TLS, and the outer tunnel must validate the server certificate. RADIUS for wireless failure after a certificate rollover is often a trust-anchor problem on the phone, not a radio-power problem. RADIUS for wireless does not choose the pairwise cipher by itself; the robust-security negotiation still has to land on a current suite such as WPA3-Enterprise.\n\nRADIUS for wireless is the wrong label for a personal SAE passphrase, even when that passphrase is long. RADIUS for wireless is the wrong label for an allow-list of phone applications, which never consults the authentication server. RADIUS for wireless change of authorization, often on UDP 3799, lets the server disconnect or reauthorize a session when group membership changes. RADIUS for wireless shared secrets should differ per controller and should not be the vendor string left in the sample config.\n\nRADIUS for wireless logs on the server should be retained off the access point so a stolen radio does not take the only copy of who joined. RADIUS for wireless test accounts belong to a lab policy and should not be a standing backdoor on the staff SSID. RADIUS for wireless dynamic VLAN assignment needs a matching trunk and a DHCP scope, or the station authenticates and then gets nowhere. RADIUS for wireless machine authentication and user authentication answer different questions, and a policy should say which one grants staff reach.\n\nRADIUS for wireless certificate templates for EAP-TLS should name the purpose, the lifetime, and who may enroll a device. RADIUS for wireless inner methods that still depend on a reversible or weakly hashed password are a reason to move those users toward certificates. RADIUS for wireless guest portals that only collect an email address are not this control, because no directory account was proven. RADIUS for wireless redundancy means at least two servers and a tested failover, since a single authentication host becomes a door stop for every radio.\n\nRADIUS for wireless timing should account for elevator lobbies and voice roam, where a slow EAP exchange drops calls even though the password was correct. RADIUS for wireless attributes must be documented so a firewall change does not strip the VLAN reply and dump staff into a dead segment. RADIUS for wireless identity strings should match the directory, and a typo in the outer identity can hide the real account from the log. RADIUS for wireless MAC filters are a weak neighbor, not a substitute, because MAC addresses are trivial to observe and replay.\n\nRADIUS for wireless onboarding should retire the old shared passphrase SSID the same week the directory method is turned up. RADIUS for wireless evidence on a ticket is an access-accept or access-reject with a user name, a NAS identifier, and a timestamp. RADIUS for wireless rejects with a quiet timeout point at reachability or the shared secret, while an access-reject points at the credential or the policy. RADIUS for wireless interim accounting helps when sessions last all day and a disconnect packet is lost.\n\nRADIUS for wireless privilege should not ride in a group that also administers the directory, or wireless support staff inherit unrelated rights. RADIUS for wireless client supplicants need a profile that pins the server name, or users will tap through a warning and train themselves to ignore it.",
        'r0': 'RADIUS for wireless places 802.1X between the station, the access point, and an authentication server that speaks the RADIUS protocol. RADIUS for wireless proves a directory identity with EAP, then returns authorization such as a VLAN in the access-accept. RADIUS for wireless accounting on UDP 1813 records the session beside the authentication on UDP 1812. RADIUS for wireless device secret authenticates the radio to the server and is distinct from the human credential. RADIUS for wireless with EAP-TLS uses certificates, while PEAP and EAP-TTLS can tunnel a password inside a validated TLS outer layer. RADIUS for wireless can send disconnect or change-of-authorization messages when the directory group changes. RADIUS for wireless evidence is the accept or reject line, not the brand of the access point.',
        'r1': 'RADIUS for wireless gives each person an account. RADIUS for wireless drops access when that account is disabled. RADIUS for wireless is not a shared passphrase. RADIUS for wireless uses 802.1X and EAP. RADIUS for wireless logs who joined and when. RADIUS for wireless can place a station in a VLAN. RADIUS for wireless likes certificates via EAP-TLS. RADIUS for wireless still needs a current cipher suite. RADIUS for wireless is not an app allow-list. RADIUS for wireless needs two servers for failover. RADIUS for wireless proof is the accept or reject.',
        'r2': 'RADIUS for wireless is the library desk that looks up your own card before it lets you into the stacks. RADIUS for wireless cancels that card the afternoon the patron leaves, and the turnstile refuses the next visit. RADIUS for wireless is not a chalkboard code shouted to every reader in the lobby. RADIUS for wireless writes a line in the checkout ledger naming the person and the hour. RADIUS for wireless can send the reader to the local-history room or the map room based on the card type. RADIUS for wireless still needs a sturdy door on the building, because a valid card does not invent a lock. RADIUS for wireless fails closed when the catalog computer is down unless a second catalog computer is ready. RADIUS for wireless proof is the ledger line, not a rumor that the building feels exclusive.',
        'r4': 'RADIUS for wireless: bind the staff name to directory accounts. RADIUS for wireless: disable a test account and confirm the join fails. RADIUS for wireless: require server-certificate checks in the supplicant. RADIUS for wireless: set a unique device secret per controller. RADIUS for wireless: log accepts and rejects off the access point. RADIUS for wireless: map each group to a VLAN and test it. RADIUS for wireless: document EAP method, servers, and failover. RADIUS for wireless: retire the shared passphrase name the same week.',
        'exam': 'RADIUS for wireless items describe 802.1X with a per-person directory proof and an access-accept. RADIUS for wireless is correct when disabling the account stops the next association. RADIUS for wireless is incorrect when the only secret is a passphrase shared by a shift. RADIUS for wireless is incorrect when the stem is about which phone programs may install. RADIUS for wireless still assumes the air suite is negotiated separately from the account check.',
    },
    'Mobile application control': {
        'body': 'Company phones stay useful when the applications on them are ones the organization selected. Mobile application control is that selection, enforced on the device. The usual pattern is an allow list of store applications the job needs, plus a refusal of sideloading, which means an install from a loose file or an unknown source. The management profile already on the phone makes the decision. It does not repair a handset whose platform protections were removed, and it does not encrypt storage. It answers a smaller question: may this package be installed.\n\nThe management service pushes the profile, and the phone enforces it at install time. An approved mail client from the company catalog proceeds. A package copied from a chat is stopped before it is unpacked when unknown sources are denied. Some fleets hide the public store and show only the company catalog, so the unapproved choice never appears. The evidence is a console row with the package name, the source, the policy name, and a result of blocked or allowed. A reviewer reads that row later. A hallway story about what the user meant is not the record.\n\nPriya in facilities tried to install a puzzle game from a file named puzzle.apk that a friend had sent. The phone refused the package. At 11:06 the console recorded device F-218, package puzzle.apk, source not the managed store, policy allow-list only, result blocked. The game never reached the home screen. Work mail, the floor map, and the work-order application stayed in place. Help desk ticket APP-318 called the event an install denial. The package never ran, and no mailbox was opened by it, so the ticket did not call the event a breach.\n\nKeep the allow list equal to the applications the work needs, leave unknown-source installs denied, and read the blocked-install report every week. When a unit needs a new tool, add that one package and name an owner for the addition. Do not lift the block for the whole building to satisfy one request. If an unapproved application is already present, remove it and find out why the profile failed to hold. A repeated refusal from one team can mean the catalog is missing a legitimate tool. That is a list problem, not a reason to abandon the list.\n\nStudents mix this control with jailbreak and root detection. An allow list limits installs on a phone whose own protections are still intact. A jailbreak or root finding means those protections were removed, and company data is then withheld. A healthy phone can block a harmless game. A rooted phone can still show approved icons. Sandboxing is a further neighbor: it confines a program that is already running. Application control decides whether the install is allowed at all. A screen lock does not choose applications.\n',
        'r0': 'Staff phones receive a management profile that names an allow list and denies every other install source. The phone evaluates the profile before a package is unpacked. In one clinic rollout the list held 14 approved applications, and unknown-source installs were set to deny. A file install then produced a console row with the package name, the clock time, and the policy result. Reviewers use that row, not a verbal report, to see what was refused. Root detection stays a separate check, because a rooted device can ignore the profile. Add a missing application by editing the list. Do not clear the deny setting for the whole fleet to finish one ticket.\n',
        'r1': 'Company phones should run only approved applications. An allow list names those applications. Sideloading means installing from a loose file. The policy blocks that path. The phone checks the rule before the install. A puzzle file from a friend is refused. The console keeps a short record of the refusal. Staff still keep the work applications they need. Add a new application by editing the list. Do not turn the block off for everyone. This check is about which applications may be installed. It is not a test for a broken phone lock.\n',
        'r2': 'A school library lends only the books that sit on its shelf list. A student who brings a random paperback from home is asked to leave it in a locker, because the librarian did not choose it and cannot vouch for it. The shelves stay useful, and the reading room stays orderly. Someone can still scribble in a borrowed book, so the shelf list is not a guard inside every page. Mobile application control is that shelf list for a phone. The allow list is the catalog, and a sideloaded file is the paperback that never receives a checkout card.\n',
        'r4': "Open the device console and read the install policy on one staff phone. Write down whether an allow list is active and whether unknown-source installs are denied. Pull yesterday's blocked rows and note the package name and the source. Do not turn the policy off to quiet a single user. If a needed application is missing, write a request to add that one package. Leave the phone's other settings alone until that request is decided.\n",
        'exam': 'An allow list is not proof the phone was left unrooted. Application control only decides which packages may be installed. A rooted phone may ignore that decision, and a healthy phone may still block a game. A blocked install is a refusal, not a breach, when the package never ran. Do not confuse this with sandboxing, which confines code already running, or with a screen lock, which stops a person who finds the phone.\n',
    },
    'Jailbreak and root detection': {
        'body': "Jailbreak and root detection notices when a phone's own protections have been stripped away, and the company policy can then withhold data. On an Apple phone, a jailbreak lifts the vendor limits so unsigned code can run with deep rights. On an Android phone, root adds a superuser path the vendor did not ship. After either change, the walls around applications and the honesty of the phone's own reports are no longer trustworthy. The check answers whether the platform is still the one you issued. It does not grade which games the user prefers.\n\nThe agent on the phone, or the management service behind it, looks for signs of that removal. Typical signs are a superuser program, a known jailbreak package, a system area that no longer matches the vendor build, or a failed attestation in which the phone cannot prove it is still the original platform. If the check fails, company mail, the remote-access client, and other managed applications are blocked. Access returns when the phone is restored from a vendor image or is replaced. Wanting one extra application does not repair the trust break.\n\nJordan's phone failed on a Tuesday morning. The compliance row read device J-77, rooted true, attestation failed, mail access denied at 09:14. Calls still worked. The work mailbox would not open, and the work applications showed a compliance block. The help desk refused a temporary bypass. The row was enough to treat the handset as untrusted until it was erased and set up again, or swapped for a spare. A screenshot of an ordinary home screen was rejected, because a rooted phone can still display ordinary icons.\n\nTurn the check on, tie it to mail and remote access, and review failures the same day. Record the device identifier and the time, and tell the user the restore path. Do not copy company files onto the phone while it is noncompliant. If many phones fail together, inspect the agent update before you assume a wave of tampering. The goal is narrow: company data stays on devices whose platform protections are still present.\n\nThe neighbor students reach for is mobile application control. That control refuses an unapproved install on a phone that is otherwise intact. This check asks whether the platform protections were removed. A blocked game on a healthy phone is an application-control event. A failed attestation is a trust event even when every visible icon is approved. Remote wipe is a different action. Detection withholds access. A wipe erases stored data. You may order both, but a blocked mailbox is not itself an erase.\n",
        'r0': "Look for a superuser binary, a known jailbreak package, or a failed platform attestation. One clinic policy treats any failed attestation as noncompliant and blocks mail on the same sync. Jordan's row at 09:14 showed rooted true and mail denied on device J-77. Restoration means a vendor image, not a promise to delete one application. In a 14-phone pilot, two failures were rooted devices, not phones that merely lacked an icon. The application allow list remained a separate setting. A phone can pass the application list and still fail this check. Write the device identifier and the attestation result before anyone discusses a bypass.\n",
        'r1': 'This check looks for a phone whose protections were removed. A jailbreak does that on one family of phones. Root does it on another. The company agent looks for signs of the change. Mail and work applications stop if the check fails. Ordinary phone calls can still work. A normal home screen does not prove the phone is safe. Restore the vendor image or replace the handset. Do not copy company files onto it meanwhile. An unapproved game on a healthy phone is a different problem. Blocking mail is not the same as erasing the phone.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic keeps medicine cabinets on hinges that alarm if someone cuts them. One morning the wires are gone and the cabinet still looks shut from the hall. The charge nurse does not keep the narcotics key in that room, even though the shelves look tidy. Fixing the hinges, or rolling in a new cabinet, comes before the key returns. A rule about which boxes may be stocked is a different rule from the alarm on the hinges. Jailbreak and root detection is that hinge alarm for a phone. Company mail is the key that stays out until the platform is restored.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the compliance row for the phone that lost mail. Write down the device identifier, the time, and whether attestation failed. Confirm mail and managed applications are blocked while the row is failed. Do not grant a bypass and do not copy company files onto that phone. Tell the user the restore path is a vendor image or a replacement. Leave the application allow list unchanged while you record this failure.\n',
        'exam': 'A failed attestation means the platform trust is gone, even if the icons look approved. An allow list on a healthy phone is a different control and can block a game without any jailbreak. A screenshot of the home screen is not a passing result. Withholding mail is not a wipe. Do not treat a rooted phone as safe because the user only wanted one extra application.\n',
    },
    'Screen locks and full-device encryption': {
        'body': "A screen lock and full-device encryption solve two different losses, and a lost phone needs both. The lock, whether a PIN, a passphrase, or a biometric that falls back to a PIN, stops a casual finder from using the screen. Full-device encryption scrambles stored data so a copy of the storage is not readable plaintext. One without the other leaves a hole. A lock on a phone that stores data in the clear can be bypassed by reading the chip. Encryption on a phone left unlocked does not stop the person holding the open screen.\n\nOn a current phone the lock secret is tied to the data key and to hardware on that device. When the screen locks after a short idle time, the key is not available to a stranger. An image of the data area, taken without the PIN, comes back as ciphertext rather than as files. Wrong guesses can be slowed, and a local policy can erase the phone after too many failures. That local erase is not a command you sent from a console. Biometrics are a convenience on top of the PIN. A swipe with no secret is not a lock, and a fingerprint setting does not prove encryption is on.\n\nA nurse lost phone P-553 on the bus. The management console showed a PIN of at least six digits, an idle lock of one minute, and encryption reported on. A finder would see the lock screen, not the clinic inbox. A lab later imaged a retired phone of the same model with encryption on and no PIN entered. The data area was not a set of readable files, and spreadsheet names were not sitting in the clear. Ticket LOSS-19 recorded both console flags. The green padlock drawn on the wallpaper was not accepted as the encryption flag.\n\nRequire the PIN length, the short timeout, and the encryption flag together. Check the console for both, because a lock screen can exist while storage is still readable. When a phone is lost, the lock and the encryption buy time until you know whether a remote command has completed. Do not accept fingerprints alone as the policy. Do not assume a laptop with a sign-in password has full-disk encryption; look up that platform's encryption state the same way. Teach people to lock the screen when they walk away, since an unlocked session has the keys available.\n\nStudents treat the lock and the encryption as one control. The lock stops casual use of the screen. Encryption stops someone who copies the storage. A phone can demand a PIN and still hold cleartext if encryption is off. Remote wipe is the neighbor that deletes data after a command. Encryption protects the data even if that command never arrives because the phone is offline. A local erase after repeated wrong PINs is still not the remote command.\n",
        'r0': "Require both a screen secret and storage encryption, then verify each flag in the console. Phone P-553 used a PIN of at least six digits, a one-minute idle lock, and encryption reported on. Without the PIN, an image of that model's data area was ciphertext, not files. A fingerprint prompt does not replace the PIN and does not prove the encryption flag. Ten wrong guesses can trigger a local erase on some policies, and that erase is not a console wipe. An unlocked phone still exposes the session to whoever holds it. Check the encryption report, not the wallpaper icon.\n",
        'r1': 'A screen lock stops a stranger from using the phone. Encryption stops a stranger who copies the storage. You want both on a lost phone. A PIN is the secret you can require. A fingerprint alone is not enough. The screen should lock after a short wait. A picture of a padlock is not proof of encryption. Check the management report for both settings. Data stays unreadable without the PIN when encryption is really on. An open screen is not protected. Erasing from far away is a different step.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic building has a lobby door and a safe in the back office. The lobby door stops a casual visitor from walking into the hallway. The safe stops someone who later pulls the file drawer out of the cabinet and carries it home. Locking only the lobby leaves the drawer readable on a workbench. Locking only the safe does nothing for a visitor who finds the office door already open. Staff who prop the lobby open all afternoon have given away the first barrier even while the safe stays shut. Screen lock is the lobby door, and full-device encryption is the safe. A remote command that empties the drawer later is a third object, not the safe itself.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the console page for the lost phone and read the lock setting and the encryption flag. Write down the PIN rule, the idle timeout, and whether encryption is on. Do not treat a wallpaper icon as the encryption flag. Do not accept biometrics alone as the control. Leave remote-wipe actions in a separate note. Stop when both settings are copied into the loss ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'The screen lock and the storage encryption are two controls. A PIN without encryption can still leave a chip readable. Encryption does not stop a person who finds the phone already unlocked. A console wipe is not the lock, and a local erase after bad guesses is not that remote command. Check the encryption flag in the report. A fingerprint prompt is not a substitute for either control.\n',
    },
    'Remote wipe': {
        'body': "Remote wipe is a command from the management console that erases company data on a phone or tablet, or erases the whole device. You send it when the hardware is lost, stolen, or kept by someone who has left and will not return it. A selective wipe removes the work profile, mail, and managed applications, and it can leave personal photos alone. A full wipe returns the device toward a factory state. Either result leaves hardware that can be set up again later. That reusable outcome is why a wipe is not the same act as shredding a disk.\n\nThe command does nothing to a device that is off or has no network until that device checks in. The console queues the order. Status of queued is not status of completed. Until the check-in, the bits remain on the storage. If a lock and encryption are already in place, those bits are not usable without the PIN. If they are not, the queue is a plan, not a finished erase. Disabling the person's account stops new sign-in. It does not clear the tablet by itself. Both actions belong in the ticket because they do different jobs.\n\nTablet serial T-1904 was stolen from a clinic cart at 14:10. The administrator issued a selective wipe at 14:22 while the tablet was offline. At 15:01 the tablet joined a network, checked in, and the console changed to wipe completed. The work mail profile was gone. Ticket LOSS-44 recorded both clocks and the completed status. The same afternoon the directory account was disabled so the password could not be used. That disablement did not produce the wipe-completed row. A tech who had only disabled the account would still have had company mail sitting on the tablet.\n\nSend the wipe as soon as loss is confirmed, choose selective or full on purpose, and watch for the completed status before you tell anyone the data is gone. Keep encryption required so the wait for check-in is not a window of readable files. Confirm the device identifier before a full wipe, because the wrong identifier erases the wrong person's device. When someone leaves, wipe or collect the hardware the same day. Do not file the ticket on a verbal promise that the tablet will be brought back next week.\n\nStudents call a queued wipe proof that the data is gone. The proof is the completed status after check-in. They also call the wipe destruction. Destruction ruins media so it cannot be used again. A wiped tablet can be enrolled again. A screen lock is not a wipe, and disabling the account is not a wipe. Sanitization is the hands-on clearing you do before you reuse media you already hold. This command is aimed at a device that may not be in the building.\n",
        'r0': 'A selective wipe removes the work profile. A full wipe resets the device. Neither finishes until the device checks in. Tablet T-1904 was reported stolen at 14:10, the selective command was queued at 14:22, and the console showed wipe completed at 15:01. Until that third time, the storage still held the data. Encryption and the screen secret cover that gap. Disabling the directory account stops sign-in and does not create the completed row. Confirm the serial before a full wipe. A completed wipe still leaves hardware that can be enrolled again, which is a different ending from shredding the storage.\n',
        'r1': "A remote wipe tells a lost device to erase data. You send it from the management console. A selective wipe removes work data. A full wipe clears the whole device. The device must be online to finish. A waiting command is not a finished erase. Encryption protects the data during the wait. Turning off the person's account does not clear the device. Watch for a completed status. A wiped device can be set up again. Smashing a disk is a different ending. Match the serial before you send a full wipe.\n",
        'r2': 'A warehouse discovers that a delivery truck has left with the wrong crates still in the back. Dispatch radios the driver to unload those crates at the next stop and send a photo when the bay is empty. Until the photo arrives, the crates are still on the truck, even though the radio call was made. Locking the warehouse door back home does not empty the truck. Cutting the crates into scrap would mean they can never be shipped again, which is a harsher act than unloading them. Remote wipe is that radio call to a device you do not have in hand. The completed status is the photo that the work data is actually gone.\n',
        'r4': 'Confirm the device serial on the loss ticket before you send anything. Choose selective or full and write that choice down. Record the time you queued the command and the time the console shows completed. Do not tell the owner the data is gone while the status still says queued. Do not treat an account disablement as the wipe. Leave encryption settings as they are while you wait for check-in.\n',
        'exam': 'A queued wipe is not a completed erase. The device must check in, and the console must show completed. Disabling the account stops sign-in and does not clear stored mail. A screen lock is not a wipe. Shredding media so it cannot be reused is destruction, while a wiped tablet can be enrolled again. Encryption covers the time before the command lands. Do not close the loss on a promise that the device will be returned later.\n',
    },
    'Sandboxing': {
        'body': "Sandboxing runs untrusted code or an untrusted document inside a confined space so a crash or a hostile payload does not take the whole computer. The program may start, but its view of files, and often its view of the network, is limited to what the confinement allows. The user's real document folder is not part of that view. The control is the boundary during the run. It is not a verdict that the file is harmless forever.\n\nThe operating system, the browser, or a mail gateway builds the boundary. That can be a restricted process, a separate account, or a disposable machine thrown away after one run. If the payload tries to read the user's documents or to plant a program that starts at login, the boundary denies the request. The denial is the result you keep. A later open outside the confinement does not inherit the promise. Giving the confined run the user's real password, or mounting the real home folder into it, punches a hole the design was meant to avoid.\n\nAlex received invoice.pdf and opened it through the mail gateway's viewer rather than on the laptop. The viewer ran in a disposable workspace that did not mount Alex's document folder. The log showed the document process attempted that folder and was denied, then the workspace was discarded. The laptop never wrote the file to disk. Ticket MAIL-902 attached the denial line and the file hash. Nobody marked the attachment safe for every future open. The hash let the team recognize the same file if it returned.\n\nOpen untrusted attachments and unknown sites inside a sandbox or an isolated browser. Do not hand that workspace real credentials or the user's home folder for convenience. Read the denial line when a file behaves badly, and keep the hash. If a product claims to confine a process but the process can still read the whole profile, the boundary is not real and the gap should be filed. Patch the sandbox tool itself. A breakout defeats the wall even when the policy text sounds strict. Keep a record of which viewer was used so the next analyst can repeat the open the same way.\n\nStudents confuse a sandbox with an application allow list. The list decides what may be installed. A sandbox confines something that is running, including an application you chose to allow. A blocked install never needs this boundary because it never starts. Jailbreak and root detection is the opposite problem: the platform walls, including the ones confinement needs, have been torn down. A screen lock does not confine a document. If the question's evidence is a denied read of the real document folder during a disposable run, the control is the sandbox.\n",
        'r0': "A mail gateway can open an attachment in a disposable workspace that is thrown away after the run. In this clinic the workspace lived for three minutes, had no mount of the user profile, and dropped its disk when the timer ended. invoice.pdf tried to read the documents folder and received a denial, recorded on ticket MAIL-902 with the file hash. The laptop never stored the file. That denial is not a finding that the file is safe to open later on the real desktop. An allow list, which decides installs, is a different control. Do not place the user's password inside the disposable workspace.\n",
        'r1': "A sandbox lets an untrusted file run in a tight space. The space cannot see the user's real documents. A mail viewer can provide that space. The file may try to reach out and be refused. The refusal is the result you save. Throw the space away after the run. Do not call the file safe forever. Do not give the space the user's password. An allow list decides what may be installed. A sandbox confines what is already running. A broken phone platform can tear this wall down.\n",
        'r2': 'A bakery tests a new filling in a small oven that is not connected to the main pantry. If the filling spills, it ruins the test tray, not the flour bins the shop will sell tomorrow. The baker still does not declare the filling wise for every future cake. Moving the test into the real pantry, because the small oven feels slow, defeats the point of the separate room. A rule about which jars may be purchased is a different rule from the test oven. Sandboxing is that separate oven for an untrusted file. The denial in the log is the spill that never reached the real documents.\n',
        'r4': "Confirm the attachment was opened in the disposable viewer, not on the laptop desktop. Write down the denial line and the file hash on the ticket. Note that the user profile was not mounted into the workspace. Do not mark the file safe for a later local open. Do not type the user's password into the viewer. Leave the application allow list for a separate review.\n",
        'exam': 'A sandbox confines a run. It does not mean the file is safe to open outside that run. An allow list decides installation and is not this boundary. A rooted or jailbroken phone can remove the walls a sandbox needs. Evidence of a denied read of the real document folder, followed by discarding the workspace, points here. Do not describe the confined payload as if it had been invited to use the real profile.\n',
    },
    'Procurement': {
        'body': "Procurement is the path by which the organization buys or subscribes, and security requirements belong in that path before the purchase order is signed. Procurement should demand that default passwords be changed, that unused services be disabled, and that the supported lifetime be long enough to patch. Procurement reviews of a camera, a badge reader, or a cloud tool should ask where data will sit, who administers it, and how it is turned off at the end. Procurement blocks surprise cloud signups by requiring a known account and a security review instead of a credit card in someone's drawer.\n\nProcurement language can require a vulnerability-disclosure contact, a software bill of materials, and a patch service-level for anything that will touch production. Procurement is not the act of labeling an asset after it arrives, and it is not the inventory row itself. Procurement is not the discovery scan that later finds the device the purchase never recorded. Procurement records should include the security checklist, the approver, and the exception if a requirement was waived.\n\nProcurement of open-source support is still a purchase decision when the organization relies on a maintainer or a paid distribution. Procurement should refuse products whose only administration channel is an obsolete clear-text protocol. Procurement for services needs a data-processing description, a subprocessors list, and an exit plan that returns or deletes data. Procurement timing matters because a clinic that buys first and asks security later inherits the vendor's defaults.\n\nProcurement pilots should use non-production data unless a signed review says otherwise. Procurement of renewed contracts is another chance to add missing requirements, not a rubber stamp of last year's terms. Procurement evidence on a ticket is the requirement in the order and the acceptance test that showed the default password was gone. Procurement coordination with legal and finance keeps security from being a surprise invoice or an unenforceable wish.\n\nProcurement standards can tier reviews so a cheap mouse does not take the same weeks as a payroll host, while anything networked still answers a short question set. Procurement should name an internal owner before the vendor's salesperson becomes the de facto owner. Procurement of hardware for resale or donation later should already say how media will be sanitized. Procurement exceptions for an urgent clinical device still need a written expiry and a compensating barrier.\n\nProcurement training for buyers stops the pattern where a department creates a tenant over a weekend. Procurement is effective when a sample order shows the security lines and the received device matches them. Procurement questionnaires should ask whether the product can disable unused listeners, export logs, and run without a shared local administrator. Procurement of a medical device should ask how long the vendor will ship security fixes after sale and what happens when that window ends.\n\nProcurement language can require the vendor to notify the organization of a vulnerability under a defined clock rather than at the vendor's leisure. Procurement reviews of open-source components inside a commercial product belong in the same checklist as the chassis, because the buyer inherits those components. Procurement should demand a configuration guide that starts from a hardened baseline rather than from an open demo. Procurement of identity-integrated tools should ask which protocols are supported and whether older clear-text binds can be turned off.\n\nProcurement acceptance can include a scan of the delivered image so a surprise listener is caught before production. Procurement files should keep the security checklist beside the contract, not in a personal folder that vanishes when the buyer changes jobs. Procurement of a renewal is the moment to remove a waiver that was meant to last one year and quietly lasted three. Procurement scoring can weight security answers so a cheap bid with no patch story loses to a bid that can be maintained.\n\nProcurement of cloud regions should state where backups are copied, not only where the primary console sits. Procurement stakeholders include the future operator, because a product security cannot administer will be administered badly. Procurement of trial tenants must expire, with data deleted, or the trial becomes an unmanaged production shadow. Procurement records of waived password rules should point at the compensating barrier that was promised in the same document.\n\nProcurement training uses one example of a bad camera buy and one example of a clean buy so new buyers see the difference. Procurement is stronger when the receiving dock will not tag an asset whose acceptance test is still blank.",
        'r0': 'Procurement puts security requirements into the buy itself: supported lifetime, no default passwords, disabled unused services, and a way to get patches. Procurement for a cloud service adds data location, subprocessors, and an exit that deletes or returns data. Procurement should stop unreviewed tenant signups. Procurement records the checklist and any waived line with an approver. Procurement is the purchase path, distinct from later classification, from the inventory row, and from a discovery scan. Procurement acceptance tests show the received product met the security lines on the order. Procurement checklists should travel with the contract and survive a buyer changing jobs. Procurement renewals are a second chance to drop a waiver that outlived its reason. Procurement acceptance can include a scan of the image that actually arrived.',
        'r1': 'Procurement puts security into the purchase. Procurement asks for patches and a supported life. Procurement forbids leaving default passwords. Procurement reviews cloud tools before signup. Procurement requires an internal owner. Procurement records any waived requirement. Procurement is not the inventory list. Procurement is not a sensitivity label. Procurement is not a discovery scan. Procurement of renewals can add missing terms. Procurement exceptions need an end date. Procurement proof is the order plus a test. Procurement asks how long fixes will ship. Procurement keeps the checklist with the contract.',
        'r2': 'Procurement is the laboratory that orders glassware only from a written spec, including lids that actually seal. Procurement refuses a pretty flask that arrives with a hole where the stopper should be. Procurement also refuses a free sample cart that a rep wheels in without the spec. Procurement writes the spec number on the receipt and notes any seal that was waived for a week. Procurement is not the tag that later names who tends the flask, and not the shelf list, and not the walk-through that finds an extra flask. Procurement of a refill order is a fresh chance to demand the seal that was missing last time. Procurement proof is a received flask whose stopper matches the spec, not a smile from the vendor.',
        'r4': 'Procurement: put security lines on the order before signature. Procurement: require default passwords to be changed before acceptance. Procurement: name an internal owner in the request. Procurement: block unreviewed cloud signups. Procurement: record any waived line with an expiry. Procurement: test one received device against the order. Procurement: add missing terms at renewal. Procurement: keep urgent buys time-boxed and fenced.',
        'exam': 'Procurement items look for security language in the buy, before the asset exists on a shelf. Procurement is correct when the order demands a changed default password and a patch path. Procurement is not the label for a sensitivity marking added later. Procurement is not the label for the master list of what is already owned. Procurement waivers without an end date are the sloppy answer.',
    },
    'Ownership and classification': {
        'body': "Every asset that matters needs a named owner and a classification. The owner is a person who can decide who may use it, how long it is kept, and whether a risk is acceptable. The classification is a handling label, such as public, internal, confidential, or restricted, that tells everyone else the rule. Accountability and handling are the two results. A label with nobody's name is a sticker. An owner with no label forces every later person to guess.\n\nThe inventory row or the data catalog stores both facts. Access requests, backup choices, and disposal methods cite the label. A restricted database is not copied onto a laptop for a demo. An internal memo can use ordinary mail. The named owner approves any narrowing of the rule. If that person leaves, the name is reassigned, because a department title cannot answer a ticket on a Friday night. The label is chosen from what the data is, not from the hostname. Encryption and access lists come after the label. They do not replace the name or the mark.\n\nPayroll database payroll-db-01 lists owner Morgan Lee, Payroll Systems, and the label Restricted, with a note that rows include employee bank details. A developer asked to copy the database onto a test laptop. The request was refused on ticket DATA-12. The refusal cited the Restricted label and named Morgan as the only person who could approve a narrower extract. The laptop never received the file. The refusal was a handling decision. It was not an outage, and it was not a claim that the database had no technical flaws.\n\nAssign a real person, put the label where the asset is listed, and teach the handling rule that follows from each label. When people change jobs, review the names within the same week. A database with a blank owner or a blank label is the finding, even if the hostname looks obvious. Do not invent a label from the server's function word alone. Ask what would happen if the rows were posted, then write the label that matches that answer. When a copy is requested, the ticket should show the label and the owner, not a shrug.\n\nStudents call the inventory itself this control. The inventory is the list of what you have. Ownership and classification are the accountable person and the sensitivity label on a row. A full list of hostnames with empty owner fields has not finished the job. Procurement is how you buy, and a purchase can require that a label be set, but the purchase order is not the label. Encryption is a control you may apply because of the label. The label is not the ciphertext.\n",
        'r0': "Put a person's name and a handling label on the asset record. payroll-db-01 is owned by Morgan Lee and marked Restricted because the rows hold employee bank details. Ticket DATA-12 refused a full copy onto a test laptop and cited that label. A department name is not an owner. Public, internal, confidential, and restricted are labels, and each one changes handling. Encryption may follow from Restricted. Encryption is not a substitute for the name. Reassign the owner in the same week a person leaves. A blank label on a complete hostname list is still an open gap. Ask what the rows contain before you invent a label from the hostname alone.\n",
        'r1': 'Every important asset needs a person and a label. The person decides access and retention. The label tells everyone how to handle it. A team name is not a person. A sticker with no name does not help at night. Restricted data does not go onto a demo laptop. The owner must approve any exception. Write both facts on the inventory row. A long list of machines is not the same thing. Buying the machine is not the same thing. Encryption may support the label. The label itself is not encryption.\n',
        'r2': "A clinic storeroom hangs a colored tag on each crate and writes one person's name on the tag. Red means the crate stays in the locked cage. Blue may ride the ordinary cart. When a tech asks to borrow a red crate for a demo, the clerk reads the tag and calls the named person. A shelf map that lists crates and leaves the tag blank does not answer the call. The purchase receipt for the crate is not the tag either. Ownership and classification are that named tag on the data. The inventory can hold the tag, but a list of bare names has not done this job.\n",
        'r4': 'Open the inventory row for the asset in the request. Write down the owner as a person and the classification label. If either field is blank, stop and record the gap. Do not copy a restricted store onto a laptop because the hostname looks harmless. Cite the label and the owner on the refusal or the approval. Do not change the label to make a demo easier.\n',
        'exam': 'The owner is a person, and the classification is the handling label. A department title is not an owner. An inventory of hostnames without those two fields is only a list. A purchase order is not the label. Encryption is not the label. The same database can be restricted because of bank details even when a test laptop would be more convenient. Blank fields are the finding.\n',
    },
    'Inventory': {
        'body': "Inventory is the maintained list of hardware, software, and data the organization actually intends to manage. Inventory is the reason a patch cycle can find a host, and a host missing from the list is a host missing from the patch cycle. Inventory rows should carry owner, location or system, version or model, and the classification already agreed. Inventory is not the same as a one-time discovery scan, though discovery is how lies in the list are found.\n\nInventory is not the purchase order, which may describe what was requested rather than what still runs. Inventory of data stores matters as much as inventory of laptops, because a forgotten file share holds the same records as the primary application. Inventory updates belong in the change path so a new server is listed before it is exposed. Inventory accuracy can be sampled by comparing a subnet discovery to the rows and explaining every extra or missing address.\n\nInventory of installed software, including libraries inside images, feeds the later question of which flaws apply. Inventory should mark assets that are decommissioned so a stale row does not look like a live gap. Inventory evidence for a finding is the missing row, the extra live host, or a version that does not match the row. Inventory tools drift, and a console that has not reported for a month is not an inventory.\n\nInventory of cloud accounts needs the same discipline, or a tenant created for a demo never enters the list. Inventory ownership is the list steward, distinct from each asset's owner, and someone must chase blank fields. Inventory of shadow spreadsheets is uncomfortable and still necessary when those sheets are the real process. Inventory retention of old rows supports investigations without pretending the asset still runs.\n\nInventory is the wrong place to hide an exception; the exception belongs in the risk record, while the asset remains visible. Inventory reviews before a penetration test or a major change stop the embarrassment of an unknown exposed service. Inventory fields for serial numbers tie later disposal certificates back to a real device. Inventory of containers and serverless functions counts as software inventory even when there is no chassis to barcode.\n\nInventory communication should tell staff how to request a row rather than plugging in an unlisted appliance. Inventory is effective when a random sample of live addresses is explained by the list and a random sample of rows is still powered or logically present. Inventory of network ranges should state which team may add a subnet, because an unlisted subnet never receives an agent. Inventory reconciliation each month should explain unknown DHCP leases rather than deleting the report.\n\nInventory of virtual machines includes the image name and the hypervisor cluster, not only an IP address that will change. Inventory of certificates and of domain names belongs beside server inventory, or expirations surprise the same team that claims the list is complete. Inventory fields for business purpose stop a row that says only Linux from hiding whether the box is payroll or a sandbox. Inventory of end-of-life software is a valid row with a flag, not a row people delete to make the report look healthy.\n\nInventory imports from the purchasing system are a feed, and they still need a human to close the loop when a device is returned. Inventory of containers should key off the image digest, because a mutable tag can hide a changed build. Inventory access should be broad enough for operators to read and narrow enough that random edits do not rewrite owners. Inventory of data stores includes the backup target, or the list describes the front door and forgets the copy.\n\nInventory sampling can pick ten serials from the loading dock and ten addresses from a switch table. Inventory errors are corrected in the list, not by silencing the discovery tool that found them. Inventory of loaner equipment needs a temporary owner and a return date so the row does not fossilize. Inventory standards should forbid duplicate serials, which usually mean a process recorded the box twice or cloned an identifier.\n\nInventory of retired rows keeps the serial for disposal matching and clearly marks that the asset is not in service. Inventory is the list a new analyst can trust on day one without an oral history from the previous analyst. Inventory of spare parts in a closet counts when those parts can be deployed without a new purchase.",
        'r0': 'Inventory is the living list of hardware, software, and data stores the organization claims to manage. Inventory rows need an owner, a version or model, a place, and a classification. Inventory feeds patching, because unlisted hosts are skipped by habit. Inventory is updated through change, and discovery is the comparison that exposes omissions. Inventory is not the purchase path and not the sensitivity decision by itself, though it stores that decision. Inventory of cloud tenants and container images counts even without a metal serial plate. Inventory proof is a sample where live systems and rows explain each other. Inventory of images should key off a digest, not a mutable tag. Inventory of unknown DHCP leases needs an explanation, not a deleted report.',
        'r1': 'Inventory lists hardware, software, and data. Inventory is what patching depends on. Inventory rows need an owner and a version. Inventory should include cloud tenants. Inventory is not a one-time discovery scan. Inventory is not the purchase order. Inventory is not the sensitivity decision alone. Inventory must be updated when systems change. Inventory goes stale if agents stop reporting. Inventory serials later match disposal records. Inventory samples compare live hosts to rows. Inventory hides nothing that is still running. Inventory includes certificate and name records. Inventory flags end-of-life software instead of hiding it.',
        'r2': "Inventory is the orchestra library's catalog of scores, stands, and borrowed instruments. Inventory tells the librarian what to repair before the tour, and an unlisted stand is a stand nobody repairs. Inventory is not the receipt from the shop that sold a stand, and not the tag that says who leads a section. Inventory is checked by walking the stage and comparing what is present to the cards. Inventory of rented scores counts, even though no warehouse shelf holds them overnight. Inventory fails when cards remain for instruments that were sold and omits the new drum that arrived Friday. Inventory proof is a walk where every sound on stage has a card and every card still has an instrument.",
        'r4': 'Inventory: record hardware, software, and data stores. Inventory: fill owner, version, place, and label. Inventory: add a row before a system is exposed. Inventory: compare discovery results to the list. Inventory: include cloud tenants and images. Inventory: retire rows when assets are gone. Inventory: sample a subnet against the rows. Inventory: keep serials for later disposal matching. Inventory: explain unknown leases instead of deleting them.',
        'exam': "Inventory items ask whether a maintained list explains the live hardware, software, and data. Inventory is correct when a missing row explains a missed patch. Inventory is not the buyer's checklist and not the sensitivity label by itself. Inventory that has not been compared to a discovery pass is a wish. Inventory of a forgotten test server is the classic miss.",
    },
    'Enumeration': {
        'body': 'Enumeration is the disciplined look at what is really present on networks and in accounts, used to test whether the inventory is telling the truth. Enumeration finds hosts, services, and identities that nobody listed, such as a webcam plugged in by a project that ended. Enumeration is authorized discovery with a scope, a time, and an owner, not a freelance scan from a laptop on a guest network. Enumeration results must be compared to the inventory row by row, and every extra or missing item needs a sentence.\n\nEnumeration of accounts finds dormant logins and groups that still grant rights after a contract ended. Enumeration is not sanitization, which removes data from media. Enumeration is not physical destruction of a disk. Enumeration is not the certificate that says disposal happened.\n\nEnumeration is not the retention schedule that says how long records stay. Enumeration evidence is the unlisted address, the unexpected open service, or the account that should have been disabled. Enumeration frequency should match how fast the environment changes, with cloud accounts checked more often than a locked wiring closet. Enumeration credentials, when used, should be a dedicated read-only path so the check does not become an admin backdoor.\n\nEnumeration output belongs in the same ticket as the inventory correction, or the list and the scan drift apart again. Enumeration of wireless names and of exposed management pages is in scope when those are assets the organization claims to control. Enumeration should avoid disruptive tests; a discovery of a service is not permission to exploit it. Enumeration after a merger is how two partial lists become one honest list.\n\nEnumeration communication tells site staff the window so a medical device is not surprised by a probe. Enumeration is effective when a previously unknown device is either added to the inventory with an owner or removed from the network. Enumeration of management interfaces should note products that still answer on a legacy protocol the standard forbids. Enumeration of cloud security groups can reveal a world-open path that the inventory diagram does not draw.\n\nEnumeration of service accounts should list where the password or key is stored, because an account with no owner is a finding of its own. Enumeration schedules in plants and clinics should avoid shift changes when a probe could be mistaken for a device failure. Enumeration results that repeat for three cycles without a list correction are a process failure, not a scanning failure. Enumeration of wireless identifiers and of captive portals belongs in the same pass when those networks carry staff traffic.\n\nEnumeration notes should separate a newly found asset from an asset that was known and merely unpatched. Enumeration handoff to the list steward should include the evidence file, not a verbal count of surprises.',
        'r0': 'Enumeration compares live hosts, services, and accounts with the inventory under an authorized scope. Enumeration turns an unlisted webcam or a dormant login into a correction, not into a exploit attempt. Enumeration output should land in the same record as the inventory edit. Enumeration is distinct from wiping media, shredding it, certifying disposal, and setting a retention clock. Enumeration credentials ought to be dedicated and limited. Enumeration proof is an extra or missing item that the list then explains. Enumeration of security groups can show a path the diagram omits. Enumeration that repeats the same surprise for three cycles is a broken process. Enumeration notes should separate a new asset from a known asset that is merely unpatched. Enumeration evidence files should travel with the list correction.',
        'r1': 'Enumeration checks what is actually present. Enumeration compares results to the inventory. Enumeration needs an authorized scope. Enumeration finds unlisted hosts and old accounts. Enumeration is not media wiping. Enumeration is not shredding. Enumeration is not a disposal certificate. Enumeration is not a retention schedule. Enumeration should not become an exploit. Enumeration results must update the list. Enumeration of mergers needs a fresh pass. Enumeration proof is the unexplained extra item. Enumeration flags legacy management protocols. Enumeration checks cloud paths against diagrams. Enumeration separates new assets from old ones.',
        'r2': "Enumeration is a keeper in a skiff counting buoys against last year's chart. Enumeration marks a buoy that is in the water and missing on the chart, and a chart mark with no buoy. Enumeration does not scrub paint off a buoy, smash a buoy, file a wreck form, or decide how many years a logbook stays on the shelf. Enumeration is done on purpose in daylight, not as a joyride. Enumeration ends when the chart is corrected or the stray buoy is pulled. Enumeration proof is the mismatch written in the margin, then cleared. Enumeration of a second pass still only corrects the chart. Enumeration does not repaint a buoy or decide its retirement date.",
        'r4': 'Enumeration: scope the look and name an owner. Enumeration: compare every hit to the inventory. Enumeration: record unlisted hosts and dormant accounts. Enumeration: use a limited credential if one is required. Enumeration: keep probes from becoming exploits. Enumeration: tell sites the window. Enumeration: correct the list or remove the stray. Enumeration: separate this pass from wipe, shred, and retention. Enumeration: attach the evidence file to the list correction.',
        'exam': 'Enumeration items are about discovering reality and correcting the list. Enumeration is not wiping, shredding, a disposal certificate, or a keep-or-delete schedule. Enumeration without a scope is the reckless distractor. Enumeration ends when the unlisted thing is owned on the list or gone from the network. Enumeration without an evidence file is unfinished.',
    },
    'Sanitization': {
        'body': 'Sanitization removes data from media so the next holder cannot read it, and so the media can be reused. A laptop can be reimaged for a new hire. A drive can return to spare stock. That reusable ending is the line that separates this work from destruction, which leaves media that cannot be used again. Dragging a file to the trash, deleting a desktop shortcut, or emptying a recycle bin does not do the job, because the contents can remain on the media for a later recovery tool.\n\nThe method has to match the media and the sensitivity. For a modern encrypted solid-state drive, cryptographic erase destroys the media key so the old ciphertext cannot be unlocked, and the drive can then be reused. Magnetic disks may need an overwrite or a purge command the drive actually supports. A single file delete is neither. After the operation, a sample of the media should not show the old file headers. Copies that can resurrect the data count too: a backup or a virtual-machine snapshot that still holds the files means the information was not fully let go. The paper that records the method is the disposal certificate, which is a separate object from the erase itself.\n\nLaptop asset 4419, an SSD that had held internal project files, was leaving to be resold. The technician ran the vendor sanitize command, a cryptographic erase, then checked that sampled sectors no longer showed headers from the old projects. The laptop was reimaged and issued to a new hire the following week. Months earlier someone had deleted a shortcut named Q3-payroll and called that a wipe. Those bytes had still been on the disk, which is why the resale process ignored the shortcut story. Ticket DISP-18 kept the asset tag, the command used, and the sample result.\n\nChoose the method from the drive type and the data label. Do not call a trash-can empty a clean drive. Do not shred a drive you intend to resell; shredding means the media cannot return to service. Hold the device in a locked cage between the last user and the finished sanitize. Match the asset tag on the work order to the tag on the laptop before you start, so the wrong machine is not erased. If the drive will not accept the command, stop and move to destruction rather than pretending the sanitize succeeded. Include the backups that would restore the same files, or the resale story is only half done.\n\nStudents swap this with destruction. Sanitization removes data and leaves media reusable. Destruction ruins the media so reuse is impossible. A second trap is the shortcut: deleting an icon is not sanitization. A third trap is the certificate. The certificate records that the work happened. The certificate is not the erase. Enumeration, which only finds systems, does not clear a disk either.\n',
        'r0': 'Clear the data with a method the media can actually complete, then reuse the device. Asset 4419 was an SSD. The technician used the vendor sanitize command, which performed a cryptographic erase, and sampled sectors showed no old file headers. The laptop was reimaged for a new hire the next week. Deleting the Q3-payroll shortcut had left the bytes in place. A backup that can restore those files must be handled too, or the data returns. If the drive will not take the command, destruction is the next path and reuse is off the table. The certificate records the method. It does not perform it.\n',
        'r1': 'Sanitization removes data so the device can be used again. Deleting a shortcut does not do this. Emptying the trash does not do this. The method must fit the kind of drive. A solid-state drive may be cleared by destroying its key. Afterward, sample the drive for old files. Also handle backups that could restore those files. Then the device may be reimaged and reissued. If the drive will not accept the clear, stop. Breaking the drive is a different ending. A paper record is not the clear itself.\n',
        'r2': 'A hotel laundry washes sheets so the next guest can sleep on the same bed. The wash has to remove what the last guest left, not merely fold the sheet so the stain is hidden. Hidden cloth that still carries the stain is not ready for the next room. Towels stored in a side closet have to be washed too, or someone will put the old ones back on the bed. A sheet that is burned cannot be used by anyone else, and that is a different decision from the wash. Sanitization is the wash that lets media return to service. Destruction is the burn, and a receipt for the laundry is not the wash cycle.\n',
        'r4': 'Match the asset tag on the laptop to the tag on the work order. Record the drive type and the sanitize method you will use. Do not accept a deleted shortcut as completion. After the command, sample the media and write down that old headers are gone. Note any backup that could restore the files. Do not shred the drive if the plan is to reuse it. Do not close the ticket if the drive refused the command.\n',
        'exam': 'Sanitization removes data and leaves media that can be reused. Destruction means the media cannot be reused. Deleting an icon or emptying a bin leaves recoverable data and is not sanitization. A backup that still holds the files undoes the story. The disposal certificate records the method and is not the erase. A drive that will not accept the command is a cue to destroy it instead.\n',
    },
    'Destruction': {
        'body': "Destruction physically ruins media so it cannot be reused. You choose it when sanitization cannot be finished, such as a drive that has failed and will not accept a wipe, or when policy says this sensitivity must not return to stock at all. After the work, nobody reformats that drive and mounts it in a server. The pieces are scrap. A format, a factory reset, and a remote wipe of a phone all leave devices that can live again. Those are different endings, and mixing the words hides whether the media still exists.\n\nThe methods are physical. Shredding, pulverizing, or incinerating leaves platters or chips that are not readable media. Chain of custody matters on the way to the machine, because the drive is still sensitive while it is intact. The serial is logged before the drive goes in. Afterward there may be nothing left from which to read a serial. A magnet is not a plan for a failed flash drive; flash does not forget because a magnet passed by. The certificate filed later is the proof that this serial met this method. The certificate is not the shredder.\n\nDrive serial WD-77H failed its health check and would not accept the sanitize command. It had held restricted archives. Facilities bagged it, wrote WD-77H on the bag, and kept the bag in a locked bin until the cross-cut shredder run. The pieces could not be reassembled into a drive. Ticket DEST-7 attached the destruction certificate listing that serial, the date, and the method shred. A coworker who suggested leaving it in a cupboard was refused. A cupboard keeps an intact drive, so the data can still come back, and the media can still be reused by someone patient.\n\nUse destruction when the wipe cannot be completed or policy forbids reuse. Record serials before the media is ruined, and match them to the certificate afterward. Store intact failed drives as sensitive assets until the run actually happens. Do not call a format, an emptied recycle bin, or a phone's remote wipe by this name. Do not send a drive you still hope to reuse into the shredder by habit. If a vendor does the work, you still match every serial. A batch certificate that omits WD-77H does not cover WD-77H.\n\nStudents say a sanitized laptop that was resold had been destroyed. It had not. Sanitization allowed reuse. Destruction means reuse is impossible. They also treat the certificate as the act. The shred is the act. The certificate is the record. A third trap is a dead drive left on a shelf because nobody could wipe it. An intact unsanitized drive is still the data. The failed command is the reason to destroy it, not a reason to stop.\n",
        'r0': 'Ruin the media when a wipe cannot finish or policy forbids reuse. Drive WD-77H failed health checks, refused the sanitize command, and had held restricted archives. It was shredded, not reformatted. The certificate on DEST-7 lists that serial, the date, and the method. Pieces from a cross-cut shredder are not a drive you can mount. A remote wipe and a cryptographic erase both leave reusable devices, so they are not this step. Log the serial before the media goes into the machine. A certificate that skips the serial does not prove this drive was included. Waving a magnet over flash memory does not clear a failed solid-state drive.\n',
        'r1': 'Destruction breaks media so nobody can use it again. Pick it when a wipe will not run. Pick it when policy says the disk must not return to stock. Shredding is one way. Pulverizing is another. Burning is another. Write the serial down before the drive is broken. Keep the drive locked until that moment. A format leaves a usable disk. A wiped phone can be set up again. Those are not destruction. The paper afterward proves the break. The paper is not the break itself. A drive left in a cupboard is still intact.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic kitchen cracks a plate that held a restricted meal, and the crack runs through the glaze. Washing cannot be trusted, because the plate will not sit safely in the rack and the glaze may hide residue. The cook breaks the plate into pieces that will not hold food again and drops them in the scrap bin. A receipt that says the plate was discarded is useful only if the pieces are actually in the bin. Folding the cracked plate into a towel and parking it on a shelf leaves a plate. Destruction is breaking storage so it cannot be reused. Sanitization would have been a wash that returned a sound plate to the rack.\n',
        'r4': 'Confirm the drive will not accept a sanitize command, or that policy forbids reuse. Write the serial on the ticket before the drive leaves your hands. Keep the intact drive locked until the shred or the incineration. Match that serial to the certificate when it returns. Do not format the drive and call the result destruction. Do not close the ticket on a cupboard location.\n',
        'exam': 'Ruined media cannot go back into service. Sanitization removes data and leaves reusable media. A resold laptop was sanitized, not destroyed. A remote wipe also leaves a device that can be enrolled again. The certificate proves the serial and the method. It is not the shred itself. A failed drive left intact on a shelf has not been destroyed, even if a wipe command failed.\n',
    },
    'Certification of disposal': {
        'body': 'Certification of disposal is the record that sanitization or destruction happened to particular media. It names the items, the method, who performed the work, and the date. It does not wipe a disk and it does not run a shredder. The record is how you prove, later, that those exact drives were handled. A spoken "we recycled them" cannot be matched to a serial next year. Without the document, an auditor cannot see which assets left or what was done to them.\n\nA recycler or an internal team produces the document. Each line carries a serial or an asset tag, a method such as cryptographic erase or shred, a date, and the vendor or operator. Before a disposal ticket closes, someone matches every serial on the pickup manifest to a serial on that document. A missing serial stays open. A certificate from a different truck does not cover this truck. The file lives with the asset record. If the method line says shred and the drive is still on a shelf, the paper is false and the shelf is the real state. Paper never changes the bits by itself.\n\nTicket DISP-220 covered drives SN4A91 and SN4A92. The recycler PDF listed both serials, the method cryptographic erase, and the date 12 March 2026. The technician compared the PDF with the pickup manifest, initialed the match, and only then closed those two lines. Drive SN4B10 had been described in a hallway as already gone and appeared on no certificate. SN4B10 stayed open and was still treated as sensitive. The closed lines stored the PDF path. A calendar invite titled recycling day was refused as a substitute.\n\nRequire the document before closure. Match serials one for one, including drives people think they remember. Reject pages that omit a method or a date. Keep the file where a later review can find it without calling someone who has left. When the vendor list and the manifest disagree, leave the ticket open. Do not sign for work you neither performed nor watched. The aim is an accurate record, not a folder that merely looks finished on Friday.\n\nStudents pick the certificate when the question asks what removed the data, and they pick the shredder when the question asks what proves disposal. Sanitization or destruction performs the work. This record proves which assets received which method, and on which date. A document that lists the wrong serials proves nothing about the drives still in the cage. A loading-dock photo with no serials is not the record either.\n',
        'r0': 'The disposal record lists serials, a method, an operator or vendor, and a date. DISP-220 closed only after SN4A91 and SN4A92 appeared on the recycler PDF with method cryptographic erase and date 12 March 2026. The technician matched both serials to the pickup manifest before those lines closed. SN4B10 had no line and stayed open. A calendar invite is not this record. The erase or the shred is the act that changes the media. The PDF is the proof that the act covered these serials. Store it with the asset file. If the method says shred while the drive is still on a shelf, believe the shelf and reopen the line.\n',
        'r1': 'A disposal certificate is the record, not the wipe. It names the drive, the method, and the date. It names who did the work. Match each serial to the pickup list. A missing serial keeps the ticket open. A spoken promise is not a certificate. The wipe or the shred did the actual work. The paper shows which items were included. File the paper with the asset record. Do not close on a calendar invite. A certificate for another batch does not count. Read the method line before you trust the page.\n',
        'r2': "A clinic keeps a log after instruments leave the washer or the scrap bin. The log does not scrub a scalpel and it does not melt one. It says which tray, which method, which person, and which morning. A nurse who says the tray was done, with no line in the log, has not finished the record. A log from last month's tray does not cover today's instruments. If the log says destroyed and the scalpel is back in the drawer, the drawer wins. Certification of disposal is that log for drives and other media. The serial match is the line that lets you close the tray.\n",
        'r4': 'Open the disposal ticket and the pickup manifest side by side. Write down every serial the manifest lists. Check each serial against the certificate, including the method and the date. Leave any unmatched serial open. Do not accept a verbal recycle claim or a meeting invite as the record. Do not mark the media destroyed or wiped if the certificate names a different batch.\n',
        'exam': 'The certificate proves disposal. It does not perform sanitization or destruction. A missing serial is an open item even if someone remembers the truck. Wrong serials prove nothing about the drives you still hold. A photo of a dock without serials is not the record. Clearing data for a later user is sanitization. When the method ruins the media, reuse is impossible. The document should say which of those methods was used, and when.\n',
    },
    'Data retention': {
        'body': 'Data retention states how long each kind of record is kept and when it must be deleted, including the copies that would bring it back. Data retention is not a slogan that everything should be kept forever in case it is useful. Data retention schedules name the record type, the start of the clock, the duration, and the deletion method. Data retention must cover backups, replicas, and mail archives, or a restore on Monday resurrects a file deleted on Friday.\n\nData retention yields to a legal hold, which pauses deletion for named matters and must be lifted when the matter ends. Data retention is distinct from enumeration, sanitization technique, physical destruction, and the disposal certificate, though deletion at the end of the clock uses those techniques. Data retention that is shorter than a regulation is a compliance miss, and retention that is far longer is a privacy and breach-cost miss. Data retention of logs should be long enough for investigations and no longer than the schedule allows.\n\nData retention owners approve exceptions in writing with an end date. Data retention implementation in the application is a feature, not a yearly manual purge that people skip. Data retention evidence is the schedule plus a sample of records older than the limit that are actually gone, including from backups. Data retention communication tells staff not to keep private shadow copies in uncontrolled drives.\n\nData retention for a departed product includes the tenant data, not only the marketing site. Data retention is effective when a dated test record disappears on schedule and cannot be restored from the usual backup. Data retention clocks should name the event that starts them, such as account closure or the last transaction, because an undefined start never rings. Data retention of chat and of ticket comments is easy to forget when the policy only mentions the file share.\n\nData retention conflicts between two rules, such as a short privacy limit and a longer financial limit, are resolved in writing before the system is configured. Data retention in a data lake must delete derived tables, not only the source file that fed them. Data retention tests should restore a backup taken before the delete and show that the aged record is removed again by the next job, or the restore becomes a bypass. Data retention of research data may be long and still finite, with a review rather than an immortal archive.\n\nData retention notices to customers should match the real schedule, or the notice is a separate defect. Data retention ownership for each line of the schedule is a named role, reviewed when that role changes hands.',
        'r0': 'Data retention sets a duration and a deletion point for each record type, and it includes backups that would undo the delete. Data retention is not indefinite storage by default. Data retention pauses for a specific legal hold and resumes when the hold lifts. Data retention that ignores replicas leaves a second copy alive. Data retention uses sanitization or destruction at the end, but it is the clock and the rule, not those techniques. Data retention proof is a record past its date that is gone from the primary store and from the restore path. Data retention exceptions need an owner and an end. Data retention clocks need a defined start event. Data retention of derived tables must follow the source delete.',
        'r1': 'Data retention sets how long data stays. Data retention also sets when deletion is due. Data retention includes backups and replicas. Data retention is not keep-everything forever. Data retention pauses for a legal hold. Data retention resumes when the hold ends. Data retention is not a discovery scan. Data retention is not the wipe technique itself. Data retention is not physical shredding itself. Data retention is not the disposal certificate. Data retention needs an owner for exceptions. Data retention proof is a record that actually ages out.',
        'r2': 'Data retention is the date stamped on a film can that says when that reel must leave the vault. Data retention also pulls the duplicate reel from the second vault, or the date is theater. Data retention is not a rule that every reel stays because film feels historical. Data retention can pause for a lawsuit about one title, then start again when the case ends. Data retention is not counting cans, washing a print, melting a print, or the form that proves the melt. Data retention proof is a can past its date that is gone from both vaults. Data retention fails when editors keep a private copy in a desk that the stamp never reaches.',
        'r4': 'Data retention: write duration and start-of-clock per record type. Data retention: include backups, replicas, and archives. Data retention: implement deletion in the system, not as a skipped yearly chore. Data retention: pause only for a named legal hold. Data retention: lift the hold when the matter ends. Data retention: forbid endless shadow copies. Data retention: test that an aged record cannot be restored. Data retention: separate the schedule from wipe technique and from certificates.',
        'exam': 'Data retention items set a keep period and a real deletion, including backups. Data retention is not enumeration, sanitization technique, shredding, or the disposal certificate. Data retention that keeps everything forever is the common wrong answer. Data retention under a legal hold is a pause, not a new forever. Data retention fails if a restore brings the record back.',
    },
    'Vulnerability scans': {
        'body': "Vulnerability scans look for known flaws and weak settings by comparing a target to signatures and configuration checks. Vulnerability scans are not exploitation, and a finding is not by itself proof that an attacker can reach the flaw from the internet. Vulnerability scans may be unauthenticated, seeing only what a remote party would see, or credentialed, logging in to read patch levels and local settings the banner hides. Vulnerability scans from an agent on the host can see software the network path cannot.\n\nVulnerability scans need a scope, a schedule, and a window so a clinical device is not probed during a procedure. Vulnerability scans produce candidates for triage, including false alarms where a banner never changed after a patch. Vulnerability scans miss issues that have no signature yet, which is why they do not replace design review or application testing. Vulnerability scans of a lab printer and of an internet-facing application are both findings, and they are not equal priorities.\n\nVulnerability scans should be tuned so safe checks are the default and disruptive checks are opt-in. Vulnerability scans evidence on a ticket includes the plugin or check identifier, the host, the port or service, and whether the scan was credentialed. Vulnerability scans after a change confirm whether a fix worked, which is a later verification step rather than the scan's identity. Vulnerability scans are not static reading of source code and are not dynamic testing that sends crafted inputs to a running application.\n\nVulnerability scans of containers should see the image contents, not only the orchestrator address. Vulnerability scans credentials must be vaulted and limited, or the scanner becomes the most dangerous account in the fleet. Vulnerability scans should cover new subnets quickly, because an unscoped acquisition is where unknown services hide. Vulnerability scans differ from a penetration test, which attempts to exploit under rules of engagement.\n\nVulnerability scans reporting to leadership should be summarized, while operators keep the raw finding list. Vulnerability scans frequency for internet-facing systems is higher than for isolated lab gear. Vulnerability scans that are never compared to the asset inventory will skip hosts the scanner cannot route to and still look complete. Vulnerability scans of default credentials and clear-text management protocols are configuration findings, still in scope.\n\nVulnerability scans do not assign a public identifier by themselves and do not compute business impact, though both may be attached later. Vulnerability scans are effective when credentialed coverage is high, new assets appear within the schedule, and findings are tracked to a decision. Vulnerability scans of authenticated Windows and Linux hosts should read the actual package database rather than trusting a banner that an administrator can edit. Vulnerability scans of network devices need a read-only account that can show the running image version.\n\nVulnerability scans schedules should stagger sites so a single scanner does not saturate a thin clinic link. Vulnerability scans of web applications inside the same program are easy to double-count with dynamic tests, and the ticket should say which tool produced the row. Vulnerability scans exclusions for a fragile device must name the device, the reason, and a compensating monitor, or the exclusion becomes a permanent blind spot. Vulnerability scans plugins should be updated before the scan, and the plugin set version belongs in the report header.\n\nVulnerability scans of IPv6 ranges fail when the scanner only walks IPv4, so dual-stack sites need an explicit check. Vulnerability scans through a firewall must be allowed on purpose, or the report describes the firewall rather than the hosts. Vulnerability scans of default community strings and of default local accounts are configuration findings worth keeping even when no CVE is attached. Vulnerability scans trend lines matter: a flat critical count can hide one team fixing issues while another team deploys new ones.\n\nVulnerability scans of golden images should happen before the image is cloned, because every clone inherits the finding. Vulnerability scans credentials that expire mid-quarter silently drop authenticated coverage, so the expiry should alarm before the password dies. Vulnerability scans of ephemeral cloud instances need an agent or a snapshot in time, or the instance vanishes and the finding vanishes with it. Vulnerability scans handoff to owners should include a reproduction note a system administrator can follow without relearning the scanner.\n\nVulnerability scans of printer fleets and of voice gateways are in scope when those devices sit on routable networks. Vulnerability scans quality reviews ask what percentage of inventory rows were actually touched, not how many pages the PDF contained.",
        'r0': 'Vulnerability scans compare systems to known flaw and configuration checks without requiring the operator to exploit the target. Vulnerability scans see more when they are credentialed or agent-based than when they only touch the network banner. Vulnerability scans are candidates for triage, not automatic proof of external reachability. Vulnerability scans need scope, schedule, and safe-check defaults. Vulnerability scans are distinct from static code reading and from dynamic input testing of a running application. Vulnerability scans also differ from a penetration test that exploits under a written agreement. Vulnerability scans proof is a check identifier on a named host with the authentication mode recorded. Vulnerability scans should record the plugin-set version in the header. Vulnerability scans exclusions need a named device and a reason, or they become blind spots.',
        'r1': 'Vulnerability scans search for known flaws and weak settings. Vulnerability scans are not exploitation. Vulnerability scans can be credentialed or not. Vulnerability scans with a login see more. Vulnerability scans need a scope and a schedule. Vulnerability scans can false-alarm on banners. Vulnerability scans miss brand-new flaws. Vulnerability scans are not static code reading. Vulnerability scans are not dynamic app probing. Vulnerability scans are not a penetration test. Vulnerability scans must cover new assets. Vulnerability scans produce findings to triage.',
        'r2': 'Vulnerability scans are a bridge inspector with a checklist walking each span on a set day. Vulnerability scans note cracks that match the pictures in the manual. Vulnerability scans do not load the span until it breaks, and a noted crack is not proof a truck can reach it from the highway. Vulnerability scans with a key to the maintenance hatch see more than a look from the riverbank. Vulnerability scans are not the architect reading drawings at a desk and not a wind-tunnel shove of a model. Vulnerability scans of a footbridge and of the main span both get notes, and the town fixes the main span first. Vulnerability scans proof is a checklist line tied to a specific span, not a shrug that bridges feel old.',
        'r4': 'Vulnerability scans: set scope, schedule, and safe checks. Vulnerability scans: prefer credentialed or agent coverage. Vulnerability scans: record the check, the host, and the auth mode. Vulnerability scans: vault the scanner credential. Vulnerability scans: add new subnets to scope quickly. Vulnerability scans: do not treat a finding as permission to exploit. Vulnerability scans: separate these results from code review and from live app tests. Vulnerability scans: compare coverage to the asset inventory.',
        'exam': 'Vulnerability scans items describe signature and configuration checks, not an exploit and not proof of reach. Vulnerability scans are not static reading of source and not crafted inputs against a running app. Vulnerability scans with credentials see a different depth than a banner-only pass. Vulnerability scans that skip new networks create a false sense of coverage. Vulnerability scans feed triage rather than replacing it.',
    },
    'Static application testing': {
        'body': 'Static application testing reads source code without running it. The checker examines the text a developer wrote and reports patterns that are dangerous. Because the application is not started, the check can run on a proposed change before anything is deployed. It finds some defects early. It cannot see every flaw that appears only when the program is in motion, and a clean result is not proof the running system is safe.\n\nThe tool parses the source and follows how data moves through functions. It can flag a database query built by gluing a user-supplied value into the command text, a secret written into a source file, or a missing check on a path the code itself reveals. It does not listen for visitors and it does not send requests to a website. The report points at a file and a line. Some reports are false alarms, because a guard in configuration or in another service may make the line safe later. A person who owns the code has to confirm the hit. Silencing the tool to make a build look green hides the line rather than fixing it.\n\nBuild 1842 of the billing service failed in the pipeline. The report named PaymentDao.java line 88, where a status value that came from the user was concatenated into a database command string. The pipeline stopped before a package was published. No billing server was started for the check. The developer rewrote the call so the value traveled as a parameter, and the next build no longer cited line 88. Ticket BUILD-1842 did not claim a customer had been attacked. It claimed the source contained a dangerous construction, and the construction was visible without executing the service.\n\nRun the check on every proposed change. Fail the build on the findings your team has agreed are blocking, and triage the rest with someone who can see the line. Fix the code. Do not waive a hit without an owner and a reason. Remember the limit: this test does not execute the application. Hand a running build to dynamic testing in a place you are allowed to disturb. A network scan that reads version banners on hosts is a third activity. It is not a reading of this source file.\n\nStudents swap this test with dynamic application testing. If the application was not running and the evidence is a file and a line, the test was static. If the service was up and someone sent inputs, the test was dynamic. A vulnerability scan of a host looks for known flaws and weak settings on the machine. It does not read PaymentDao.java. A static hit is also not a severity score and not, by itself, a public flaw identifier. The identifier is a name some flaws receive later. This report is a line in the source.\n',
        'r0': 'Read the source and do not start the application. Build 1842 failed because PaymentDao.java line 88 concatenated a user-supplied status into a database command string. The pipeline stopped before publish, and no billing process was launched for that check. The repair passed the value as a parameter, and the following build no longer cited line 88. A clean static run still misses flaws that appear only while the service handles a live request. Dynamic testing is the run against a started application. A host scan that matches banners is neither. Treat a hit as a line to confirm, because some pattern matches are not reachable defects.\n',
        'r1': 'This test reads the source code. The application stays off. It can spot a query built by gluing text. It can spot a secret written in a file. The report names a file and a line. Run it before you ship the change. A clean result is not proof the live app is safe. Some hits are false alarms. A person should confirm the line. Testing a running app is a different test. A scan of server banners is a different test. Do not silence the tool just to look finished.\n',
        'r2': 'An architect reads the drawings at a desk before anyone turns the oven on in a new kitchen. A beam drawn across a doorway is visible on paper, and the beam can be moved while the page is still cheap to change. The reading cannot feel a draft that appears only after the doors are hung. Sometimes the architect circles a mark that a cook knows is a symbol, not a mistake, so a person still decides. Blueprints are not a taste of soup that is already boiling. Static application testing is that reading of source while the program is not running. The cited line is the mark on the drawing, not a bowl that was served.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the failing build report and write down the file name and the line number. Confirm the application was not started for this check. Read the cited line with the author before you call the hit real. Do not waive the finding without an owner. Do not publish the package while a blocking hit is still open. Leave live testing of a running build as a separate step.\n',
        'exam': 'Static testing reads source code and does not run the application. Dynamic testing sends inputs to a running application. A banner scan of a host is neither one. A file-and-line hit is not a severity score and is not automatically a public flaw name. Some static hits are false alarms and need a person. Silencing the check to force a green build hides the line. A clean static run does not retire a test of the running service.\n',
    },
    'Dynamic application testing': {
        'body': "Dynamic application testing tests a running application. The program is started in a place you are allowed to test, and a person or a tool sends inputs, follows links, and watches the responses. Flaws that exist only in motion can show up here, including a check that the live request path simply skips. Reading the source is a different test. This one does not open the source file to do its work. It judges behavior of a build that is actually up.\n\nThe tester needs a running instance, accounts for the roles that matter, and a boundary on how hard to push. The tool crawls pages and submits crafted values. It watches for another person's data in a response, an error that reveals too much, or a control that says yes when it should say no. Findings reflect this build, this configuration, and this identity. A hit is evidence from behavior. It still needs confirmation, because test data can create a false alarm. Pointing the same activity at production, when the test changes data or can knock the service over, is how a safe check becomes an outage.\n\nOn the staging site, a test account submitted a search with a crafted identifier. The running application returned invoice INV-10091, which belonged to a different customer. The report stored the search page, the parameter that changed, and a short piece of the response. The application process stayed up the whole time. Nobody opened the source as part of this test. Ticket APP-551 named the staging build and forbade the same probe against production. A second try from the same test account reproduced the other customer's invoice, so the result was not a one-time glitch in the log.\n\nAim dynamic tests at a sanctioned environment. Give them accounts so they see the pages real users see. Schedule them against the build you intend to ship. Confirm a hit by repeating the request once, then fix the application and run the same test again. Do not treat a quiet run as permission to skip reading the source, and do not treat a source review as a substitute for this run. A vulnerability scan of the host operating system looks for known flaws and weak settings on the machine. It does not prove the application's own request logic is sound.\n\nStudents swap the two application tests. If the service was not running, the test was not dynamic. If nobody executed the program and the tool only read files, the test was static. Evidence for this topic is a request, a response, and a build that was up. A host scan that never sent the crafted search is a different activity. A false alarm from messy test data is still not a real defect, and a real defect the tool never sent is a miss, not a passing grade.\n",
        'r0': 'Exercise a running application with inputs and watch the responses. On staging, a test account sent a crafted search and the live process returned invoice INV-10091 for a different customer. The report kept the page, the parameter, and a response snippet. Ticket APP-551 tied the hit to that staging build and kept the probe off production. The source file was not opened for this test. Repeat the request once before you call it real. Static reading of source, with the process stopped, is the other application test. A host vulnerability scan that only matches versions does not walk this request path.\n',
        'r1': "This test uses an application that is already running. You send inputs and watch what comes back. The source file stays closed for this test. Use a safe copy of the app, not the live business. A search that returns someone else's data is a real clue. Write down the page and the response. Try the same request once more. Then fix the app and test again. Reading code while the app is off is different. Scanning a server banner is different. A quiet run does not replace those other checks.\n",
        'r2': "A cook tastes the soup while it is on the stove. The taste can reveal too much salt that a written recipe card did not make obvious. The tasting happens only because the pot is hot and the soup is moving. Reading the card in the office, with the burner off, is a different chore. Dumping test spoons into the pot that is about to be served to the dining room can ruin dinner. The cook writes down which pot and which spoonful before changing the salt. Dynamic application testing is that taste of a running program. The other customer's invoice in the response is the spoonful that should never have been in this bowl.\n",
        'r4': 'Confirm the target is the sanctioned running build, not production. Record the page, the input you changed, and what the response contained. Repeat the request once and write down whether it happens again. Do not open the source and call that this test. Do not close the ticket before the same request is tried on the fixed build. Leave host version scans in a separate note.\n',
        'exam': "Dynamic testing requires a running application and inputs sent to it. Reading source without starting the program is static testing. A host scan for known flaws is not either application test. One reproduced response that shows another customer's data is evidence of behavior, not a source line. Keep destructive or data-changing probes off production. A quiet dynamic run does not prove the source was reviewed.\n",
    },
    'Package monitoring': {
        'body': "Package monitoring watches libraries and packages the organization already uses and alerts when a newly announced flaw matches one of those versions. Package monitoring reads lockfiles, image manifests, and dependency graphs, including transitive packages the developers did not type by hand. Package monitoring is what fires the morning a library flaw is published and the billing service still vendors that version. Package monitoring is not a live host scan, though a host scan may later confirm the package is loaded.\n\nPackage monitoring is not a threat feed of malicious addresses, and it is not a review of public job posts. Package monitoring should name the package, the version, the advisory, and the applications that include it. Package monitoring of container base images matters because the vulnerable bits may sit under the application code. Package monitoring noise drops when versions are pinned and when the graph is generated from the build rather than from a guess.\n\nPackage monitoring must see private registries, or an internal fork hides from the public advisory match. Package monitoring severity is an input, and reachability inside the app still decides urgency. Package monitoring exceptions for a package that cannot move this week need an owner and a date. Package monitoring should distinguish a build-time tool from a library shipped to customers.\n\nPackage monitoring evidence is the advisory identifier, the version range, and the repo or image that contains the hit. Package monitoring after a bump means the next build no longer resolves to the bad version. Package monitoring fails open when a team vendors a zip file that never enters the manifest. Package monitoring of firmware and of phone apps is the same idea with a different manifest.\n\nPackage monitoring cadence is continuous, because advisories do not wait for a quarterly change window. Package monitoring ownership sits with the team that ships the app, with a central view so executives see aging exceptions. Package monitoring does not by itself test the running request path; that remains dynamic testing or a host scan. Package monitoring of license changes can ride along, but a license surprise is not a vulnerability finding.\n\nPackage monitoring integration should open a ticket once per package per application, not a mail storm per developer. Package monitoring baselines help a new repository start clean without hiding later advisories. Package monitoring is effective when a deliberately old test dependency produces an alert the same day the advisory is added to the feed the tool uses. Package monitoring of transitive dependencies should show the parent that pulled them in, so the fix is a direct bump or an override rather than a guess.\n\nPackage monitoring ignore files are as sensitive as scanner suppressions and need an expiry. Package monitoring of language ecosystems differs: a lockfile in one ecosystem is a hash pin, and a range specifier in another will float. Package monitoring should fail a release when a forbidden severity is newly present, while older debt can be a timed exception. Package monitoring of container bases should rebuild on a cadence even when application code did not change, because the base moves.\n\nPackage monitoring advisories that are withdrawn should close the ticket automatically so teams do not chase a retracted issue. Package monitoring of development-only packages can be a lower tier when those tools never ship, and the tier must be explicit. Package monitoring reachability analysis, when the tool offers it, is a hint, not a proof, until someone confirms the vulnerable function is callable. Package monitoring across microservices needs a portfolio view so the same library bump is coordinated rather than done twelve times in twelve ways.\n\nPackage monitoring of firmware images for appliances belongs if the vendor publishes a version the tool can compare. Package monitoring during an incident can answer quickly whether a named package is present, which is a different question from the daily hygiene queue. Package monitoring mirrors should be the source of truth the build uses, or the monitor and the build disagree. Package monitoring of a forked library should track the fork's commits, not only the upstream advisory that the fork may never merge.\n\nPackage monitoring reports to leadership work as aging of exceptions, not as a raw count of transitive rows. Package monitoring onboarding of a new repository is incomplete until the first build produces a graph. Package monitoring of a bill of materials export should match the graph the build already produced.",
        'r0': 'Package monitoring matches announced flaws to the libraries and images the organization builds, including transitive dependencies. Package monitoring depends on real manifests and lockfiles rather than a remembered list. Package monitoring names the version, the advisory, and the affected application. Package monitoring is not a malicious-address feed and not a public-source review of job posts. Package monitoring urgency still depends on whether the vulnerable code is reachable. Package monitoring proof is a hit that disappears from the next build after the version bump. Package monitoring misses zip files and forks that never enter the graph. Package monitoring ignore files need an expiry, just as suppressions do. Package monitoring of a fork should follow the fork, not only the upstream notice. Package monitoring mirrors must match the build or the two stories diverge.',
        'r1': 'Package monitoring watches libraries you already use. Package monitoring reads lockfiles and image manifests. Package monitoring includes transitive dependencies. Package monitoring alerts when a new flaw matches. Package monitoring names the version and the app. Package monitoring is not a malicious-address feed. Package monitoring is not a review of public posts. Package monitoring is not a live host scan. Package monitoring still needs a reachability decision. Package monitoring exceptions need an owner and a date. Package monitoring must see private registries. Package monitoring is checked by a deliberate old dependency.',
        'r2': 'Package monitoring is a pharmacy watching lot numbers on the shelves against the morning recall board. Package monitoring includes the ingredient hidden inside a compound, not only the jar the pharmacist typed. Package monitoring rings when a recalled lot is actually on site. Package monitoring is not a list of suspicious street addresses and not a tourist brochure that reveals the shop layout. Package monitoring of a jar that was poured into an unlabeled tin fails, because no lot number remains. Package monitoring after a swap shows the new lot on the board and the old lot gone. Package monitoring proof is a recall line tied to a real jar, then a shelf that no longer holds it.',
        'r4': 'Package monitoring: generate graphs from builds, not from memory. Package monitoring: include transitive dependencies and base images. Package monitoring: alert with package, version, advisory, and app. Package monitoring: include private registries. Package monitoring: time-box exceptions with an owner. Package monitoring: bump the version and confirm the next build is clean. Package monitoring: do not treat a zip drop outside the manifest as invisible on purpose. Package monitoring: separate this watch from address feeds and from public-post reviews.',
        'exam': 'Package monitoring items match a new library flaw to a version the organization already ships. Package monitoring is not a feed of bad network addresses and not open-source intelligence about public posts. Package monitoring without transitive dependencies understates exposure. Package monitoring that cannot see a private registry is blind there. Package monitoring closure is a clean build, not a promise to upgrade later.',
    },
    'Threat feeds': {
        'body': "Threat feeds are streams of indicators, such as addresses, domains, URLs, and file hashes, that a defender can use to detect or block. Threat feeds are useful only with a judgment about false-alarm rate, age, and whether the indicator matches this environment. Threat feeds can arrive as a commercial subscription or as a community share, and the redistribution rules of the share still apply. Threat feeds that are dumped straight into a block list can break a business partner when a shared host or a content-delivery address is listed.\n\nThreat feeds should record why an indicator was trusted, when it expires, and which control consumes it. Threat feeds are not package monitoring, which watches your libraries, and they are not open-source intelligence gathered from public documents. Threat feeds of file hashes help endpoint tools, while address feeds help firewalls and resolvers, and mixing those types in one control is a parsing mistake. Threat feeds need a review when the source suddenly grows tenfold, because quantity is not quality.\n\nThreat feeds evidence on a ticket is the indicator, the source, the sighting time, and the local log line that matched. Threat feeds should be tested with a canary indicator the team controls, so a broken ingest is noticed. Threat feeds aging policy removes stale entries that would otherwise block an address reused by an innocent tenant. Threat feeds do not declare an incident by themselves; a match is a lead until a person or a correlated log agrees.\n\nThreat feeds for phishing URLs change quickly and need a shorter lifetime than a hash of a stable tool. Threat feeds integration should survive a source outage by keeping the last good set for a defined time, then alarming. Threat feeds sensitivity differs for a clinic that blocks aggressively and a lab that only alerts. Threat feeds must not include indicators the organization is forbidden to redistribute into a public ticket.\n\nThreat feeds of command names that are too generic, such as a common shell, create noise rather than detection. Threat feeds ownership includes someone who can pause a bad source the same day. Threat feeds comparison across two sources helps when one is noisy and the other is slow. Threat feeds are effective when a planted test indicator alerts or blocks, and when a known-benign partner address is not stuck on the list after its listing expires.\n\nThreat feeds of IP addresses should be matched with care on shared hosting and on content-delivery networks, where one address serves many customers. Threat feeds of domains can be safer to block than addresses when the domain is attacker-controlled and the address is not. Threat feeds ingested in STIX or a simple CSV still need a mapping so a hash is not loaded into an address list. Threat feeds overlap should be measured: two sources that always agree add little, and two that never agree need an explanation.\n\nThreat feeds used for mail filtering should not include the organization's own sending domains. Threat feeds consumer systems should log the source identifier on every block so a later outage can be traced to a bad entry. Threat feeds review meetings can retire a source whose confirmations stay near zero for a quarter. Threat feeds of hashes for commodity tools will collide with administrator software if the hash is too generic a utility.\n\nThreat feeds timing for a fast phishing campaign is hours, not the weekly change window used for firewall hygiene. Threat feeds legal terms may forbid sending the indicators into a ticketing tool that contractors can read. Threat feeds enrichment with local asset ownership turns a bare address into a decision. Threat feeds of TOR exit nodes or similar broad categories are policy choices, not automatic malice.\n\nThreat feeds testing in a lab should show both a hit and a non-hit before production enforcement. Threat feeds rollback plans matter when a bad publish blocks a critical partner. Threat feeds documentation lists the consumer, the action, the expiry default, and the person who can pause the source. Threat feeds are a supplement to local detection content the team writes from its own incidents.\n\nThreat feeds paused for a partner outage should record who paused them and when they must be restored. Threat feeds of newly registered domains can be noisy and still useful as an alert rather than as a hard block.",
        'r0': 'Threat feeds supply indicators such as addresses, domains, URLs, and hashes for detection or blocking. Threat feeds require a measured false-alarm rate, an expiry, and a named consumer. Threat feeds can disrupt partners if a shared address is blocked without review. Threat feeds are not library monitoring and not research in public documents. Threat feeds matches are leads until local evidence agrees. Threat feeds need a canary to prove ingest works and an aging rule so stale entries leave. Threat feeds proof is a sourced indicator tied to a local log line. Threat feeds on shared hosting addresses need extra care before a block. Threat feeds should log the source identifier on every enforced hit. Threat feeds rollback must exist before enforcement, because a bad publish will eventually arrive.',
        'r1': 'Threat feeds stream indicators for detect or block. Threat feeds include addresses, domains, URLs, and hashes. Threat feeds need an expiry and a source. Threat feeds can false-alarm. Threat feeds can break partners if applied blindly. Threat feeds are not library monitoring. Threat feeds are not public-document research. Threat feeds matches are leads, not automatic incidents. Threat feeds need a canary test. Threat feeds must age out stale entries. Threat feeds should pause a bad source quickly. Threat feeds proof is a local log match.',
        'r2': 'Threat feeds are the harbor notices that list shoals reported by pilots this week. Threat feeds help the next boat only if the notice says who reported the shoal and how old the report is. Threat feeds that nail a permanent chain across the whole channel will also stop the ferry. Threat feeds are not a recall board of medicine lots and not a tourist map of the town. Threat feeds of a shoal that was dredged last season should come off the board. Threat feeds proof is a notice plus a sounding from your own boat, not the notice alone. Threat feeds fail when every scrap of gossip is carved into the board with no date.',
        'r4': 'Threat feeds: record source, indicator type, and expiry. Threat feeds: measure false alarms before a hard block. Threat feeds: separate address lists from file hashes. Threat feeds: plant a canary and confirm ingest. Threat feeds: age out stale indicators. Threat feeds: pause a source that suddenly floods. Threat feeds: treat a match as a lead until local logs agree. Threat feeds: keep redistribution limits in mind.',
        'exam': 'Threat feeds items are streams of indicators used with judgment, not automatic guilt. Threat feeds are not package monitoring and not open-source intelligence. Threat feeds dumped into a permanent block list are the risky distractor. Threat feeds without expiry accumulate stale damage. Threat feeds value shows up when local evidence matches the indicator.',
    },
    'Open-source intelligence': {
        'body': 'Open-source intelligence is what anyone can learn from public sources. Job posts, public code, talks, news, documents that are already public, and services your organization itself exposes all count. You do not break into a system to collect it. The material is already outside. Attackers read it to choose a target and a story. Defenders read the same material to see what the organization has given away. The collection is not a purchased list of bad addresses, and it is not a search of criminal markets.\n\nYou search the way a stranger would. A job post that names a badge product, a public repository that contains an internal hostname, or a forgotten test site that answers from the internet is a disclosure finding. You record the page address and the date, because public pages change and a later denial needs the copy you saved. Nothing in this work requires a stolen password. The result is a fact about what was published. Treating every public page as a confirmed breach overstates it. Treating a detailed post as harmless because "it is only hiring" understates it.\n\nThe careers site posted a role for a badge administrator and named the exact reader product used on the lobby doors. The page was the company\'s own. An attacker would use that sentence to learn what to study and what uniform story to imitate. Nora copied the page address and the sentence into ticket OSINT-14 on 4 April 2026, then asked recruiting to remove the product name. Facilities confirmed the product was real. No system was broken that day. The information had been published by the organization. The fix was a quieter posting, plus a pass over the other open roles for the same kind of detail.\n\nRead your public posts, public code, and exposed services on a schedule. Remove details that help an intruder and do not help a candidate or a customer. Store the source with the date, because an accusation without the page is a rumor. Describe the outsider\'s reading as the way the information is abused. Describe the review and the removal as the defense. Do not call a hired stream of indicators this practice. That stream is a feed someone else curated. Do not call a criminal-market listing this practice either. That sighting is handled as a lead under dark-web monitoring.\n\nStudents label every research task a threat feed, or every public sentence a breach. A feed is a stream of indicators, often addresses, prepared for blocking or detection. A package watch tells you a library you use has a newly announced flaw. Open-source intelligence is what you gather from public sources, including your own postings. A job ad is not a feed, and a job ad is not, by itself, proof that someone already walked through the lobby.\n',
        'r0': 'Gather what a stranger can already see, and record the page and the date. On 4 April 2026 the careers site named the lobby badge-reader product in a job ad. Ticket OSINT-14 stored the address and the sentence, and recruiting removed the product name. Facilities agreed the named product matched the lobby doors. No intrusion was required to read the ad, and no intrusion was claimed. A feed of addresses to block is a different supply. A watch on third-party libraries for new flaws is a different supply. A criminal-market listing is not a careers page. Keep the saved page, because the live site will change.\n',
        'r1': 'This work uses information anyone can already see. Job posts count. Public code counts. Your own exposed services count. You do not break in to read them. Save the page and the date. Attackers use the same pages to pick a target. Defenders use them to take secrets down. A hiring ad that names a door lock is a leak. Removing that detail is the fix. A bought list of bad addresses is different. A sighting in a criminal market is different. A public page is not automatically a break-in.\n',
        'r2': "A shop tapes notices on the front window: the hours, the alarm brand, and a help-wanted card that names the lock on the back door. Anyone on the sidewalk can read the card. A thief uses it to practice a story. The owner uses the same card as a reason to reprint it without the lock's name. The sidewalk reading is not a raid, and it is not a private phone call from another shop. Buying a list of suspicious cars from a security service is a different habit. Open-source intelligence is that sidewalk reading of public notices, including your own. The saved copy of the card is what you keep after the window is changed.\n",
        'r4': 'Open the public page that revealed the detail and save the address and the date. Write down the exact sentence that names the product, tool, or hostname. Ask the team that published it to remove the detail. Do not mark an intrusion just because the page was public. Do not delete the saved copy after the live page changes. Leave feeds and criminal-market reports for their own tickets.\n',
        'exam': 'Public sources, including your own job posts and exposed services, are this practice. A curated stream of bad addresses is a threat feed. A watch for new flaws in libraries is package monitoring. A criminal-market hit is dark-web monitoring and stays a lead until checked. Reading a careers page is not itself a break-in. The defense is to reduce what the page reveals and to keep a dated copy.\n',
    },
    'Information-sharing organizations': {
        'body': 'Information-sharing organizations are groups in which peer organizations warn one another about threats. A health group, a finance group, or a regional group circulates campaign descriptions and indicators. Membership comes with rules about what you may repeat, whom you may name, and how far a note may travel. The value is an earlier warning than you would get alone. The duty is to honor the limit that arrived with the warning. A note in your inbox is not automatically public, and it is not automatically proof that you were hit.\n\nA peer describes a campaign: a subject line, a file hash, or a behavior. The note carries a sharing limit. Your team may use the indicator in detection. Your team may not paste the peer\'s name, and sometimes may not paste the raw note, into a public chat or a blog. The detection rule and the gossip are different acts. A share is also not a confirmed incident in your environment until you search your own logs. You can quarantine matches you find. You still do not thank the sender by announcing their name to the company.\n\nA peer clinic, through the sector sharing group, warned of a phishing campaign and asked that the sender not be named outside member organizations. The note included one subject line, "Invoice past due for ward 4." The receiving team added that subject to the mail filter. Ticket SHARE-6 said the source was a peer share and that the peer must not be named. Three messages in the local mail logs matched the subject and were quarantined. The clinic\'s name never entered the company-wide chat. The indicator was used. The confidentiality that came with it was kept.\n\nJoin the sharing body that matches your sector. Read the limit before you forward anything. Record the restriction on the ticket so the next person does not "help" by posting the source. Contribute warnings back when your own rules allow, with the same care about names. Do not treat a peer note as open-source material just because it was emailed to you. Do not declare a breach from the note before your logs agree. If the limit says the indicator stays inside the organization, a vendor status page is the wrong place for it.\n\nStudents call this public research. Open-source intelligence uses material that is already public and carries no peer confidentiality. A sharing-group note can be restricted even though it looks like ordinary mail. Dark-web monitoring searches criminal markets. It is not a warning a peer volunteered. Students also treat the peer\'s indicator as a confirmed incident. It is a lead until your own systems show the activity. Posting the sender\'s name to prove you are transparent can violate the share you were trusted with.\n',
        'r0': 'Use a peer warning inside the limit that came with it. A health-sector note marked TLP:AMBER described a phishing subject, "Invoice past due for ward 4," and forbade naming the sender in public. TLP:AMBER limits redistribution to the organization and to clients who need the indicator. Ticket SHARE-6 recorded that limit. The mail filter took the subject line. Three local messages matched and were quarantined. The sending clinic was not named in the company chat. The note was not treated as proof of a breach before the log search. Public job ads and criminal-market reports follow different rules. Keep the sender\'s name off public status pages as well.\n',
        'r1': "Peers in a sharing group warn each other about threats. The warning comes with rules on what you may repeat. You may use the clue in your own tools. You may not post the sender's name in public. A subject line can be enough to search your mail. Hits in your logs matter more than the rumor. Quarantine the messages you actually find. Do not announce a breach from the note alone. Public web pages are a different source. Criminal markets are a different source. Write the sharing limit on the ticket. The next person must see it.\n",
        'r2': "Two clinics on the same street call each other when a bad batch of supplies is rumored. The caller asks that the clinic's name stay off the town notice board. The receiving clinic can still pull matching boxes off its own shelf. Shouting the caller's name in the square would break the ask, even if the shout feels honest. Reading a poster already glued to the square is a different act, because the poster was public. Certification logs and shredders are not this phone call. Information-sharing organizations are that peer call, limit included. The quarantined messages are the boxes you actually found on your own shelf.\n",
        'r4': "Read the sharing limit on the peer note before you forward it. Write the limit and the indicator on the ticket, not the peer's name in a public channel. Search your own logs for the indicator. Record how many matches you found and what you did with them. Do not declare an incident from the note alone. Do not paste the raw note into a company-wide chat.\n",
        'exam': 'A peer warning under a repeat-limit is this topic. Public pages are open-source intelligence and carry no peer confidentiality. A criminal-market listing is dark-web monitoring. The indicator is a lead until your logs confirm it. Naming the sender in a public chat can break the share even when you use the indicator correctly. TLP:AMBER is not a license to post the note on a public page.\n',
    },
    'Dark-web monitoring': {
        'body': 'Dark-web monitoring searches criminal markets and dump sites for your data or your names. A service or an internal team reports a sighting: mail addresses, a password sample, a document, or a mention of the organization. The report is a lead. It is not, by itself, a declaration that you were breached. Someone has to check whether the sample is real, whether it is current, and whether it came from you. Old dumps are reposted for years, and a date printed on a listing can be a sales pitch.\n\nThe service matches posts to domains or names you care about, then analysts verify. Do the addresses exist in the directory. Does the password sample match a current secret. The identity team can answer that without pasting the secret into a team chat. Is the document actually yours, or a forgery made to advertise a sale. Verification separates a sighting from an incident. Buying more stolen data to "see the rest" is not a verification step, and talking to the seller can tip them that the sample mattered.\n\nAt 08:40 a monitoring report listed three company mail addresses and a partial password sample from a market post. Two addresses belonged to current employees. The identity team compared the sample with current credential stores and reported that it matched no active secret. Mail logs for those two people showed no suspicious sign-in that week. The third address was not in the directory at all. Ticket DW-30 stayed a verification task. It did not become a breach declaration. The sample was stored in the restricted case file, not in the team channel.\n\nTreat every hit as a lead. Check identity, freshness, and impact before you escalate the language. If the sample is real and current, disable the affected accounts to contain credential use, and keep the report as evidence before cleanup. If the sample is stale or fake, close the lead with the reason written down. Do not confuse a sighting with a public job post. That post is open-source intelligence gathered from ordinary pages. Do not confuse it with a peer clinic\'s warning under sharing rules. A market listing was not volunteered by a partner.\n\nStudents read a monitoring hit as proof of a breach. The hit is a lead until it is verified. A false positive here is an alert that is not a real problem, such as a reposted old dump or a fake listing. A false negative would be a real dump the service never showed you, which is a different failure. Neither outcome is a severity score, and neither is a public flaw identifier. The identifier names a software flaw. This report names a sighting that still needs a check.\n',
        'r0': 'Treat a criminal-market sighting as a lead, then verify it. At 08:40 the report listed three mail addresses and a partial password sample. Two addresses were real employees. The sample matched no active secret, and those mailboxes showed no suspicious sign-in that week. The third address was not in the directory. Ticket DW-30 stayed a verification and did not declare a breach. The sample stayed in the case file. Disabling accounts is appropriate when a sample is real and current, and that disablement contains credential use. A careers-page leak and a peer-sharing note are different sources. Do not contact the seller to buy a larger dump.\n',
        'r1': 'This watch looks for your data in criminal markets. A hit is a lead, not an automatic breach. Check whether the addresses are real. Check whether a password sample is current. Ask the identity team to do that comparison. Keep the sample out of group chat. Old dumps are sold again for years. A fake listing is not a loss. If the sample is real, lock the affected accounts. If it is stale, write down why you closed it. A public job ad is a different kind of find. A note from a peer clinic is a different kind of find.\n',
        'r2': "Someone pins a rumor on an alley board saying the clinic's patient list is for sale, and they tape a corner of a page as proof. The charge nurse does not announce a loss over the loudspeaker. She checks whether the corner matches a real page and whether the names still work here. A corner from a decade-old booklet is not today's loss. Paying the rumor's author for a thicker stack is not how the clinic checks facts. A poster on the clinic's own front window is public in a different way. Dark-web monitoring is that alley board. Verification is the comparison that decides whether the corner is a real current page.\n",
        'r4': 'Open the monitoring report and list each address or sample it claims. Check which addresses exist in the directory. Have identity staff compare any secret sample with current credentials. Write down whether mail logs show suspicious sign-ins. Do not declare a breach before those checks. Do not paste the sample into a team channel, and do not contact the seller.\n',
        'exam': 'A market sighting is a lead, not an automatic breach declaration. Verify that the sample is real and current before you change the incident language. A reposted old dump or a fake listing is a false positive, an alert that is not a real problem. A real dump the service never reported would be a false negative. Disabling affected accounts, once the sample is confirmed, contains credential use. Do not call the sighting a public web page or a peer-sharing note.\n',
    },
    'False positives and false negatives': {
        'body': "A false positive is an alert that is not a real problem. A false negative is a real problem with no alert. The two failures point in opposite directions. One cries wolf. The other stays quiet while something is actually wrong. You track them separately because the repairs are different. Tuning that only reduces noise can hide more real events. Tuning that alerts on everything buries the true cases in junk. A detector that is never checked for both mistakes will be praised for the wrong reason.\n\nTools decide from signatures, version strings, banners, and rules. A banner that still shows an old version after the software was patched raises an alert for a flaw that is already gone. That alert is a false positive. A flaw the tool has no check for produces no row. If the flaw is real, the silence is a false negative. Closing an alert without looking can turn a true problem into a missed one if you were wrong about it. Ignoring quiet systems because they produced no row is how false negatives survive. A true positive is a real problem that did alert. A true negative is a quiet system that is actually fine. Those two mean the detector worked.\n\nMonday's scan flagged host 10.4.2.20 for a vulnerable library. The package was already patched. The banner string had never been updated, so the scanner matched the old text and raised the alert. The analyst confirmed the patch level with the package manager and closed the alert as a false positive, with the banner named as the cause. The same week a tester found an authorization bug on host 10.4.2.40. The scanner had no plugin for that bug, so the scan showed nothing. That empty result was a false negative: a real problem with no alert. Both outcomes were written into the weekly note, in separate sentences, so nobody could swap the labels later.\n\nConfirm an alert before you call it an incident, and record why a closed alert was not a real problem. Sample systems that produced no alerts, using a second method such as a hands-on test, so silence is not mistaken for health. Measure both rates. Do not delete every noisy rule on a Friday without asking which true problems that rule still catches. When you brief leaders, say which items were false positives and which gaps were false negatives. The phrases are not interchangeable, and a low score on a real flaw does not rename it.\n\nStudents swap the pair. An alert that is not a real problem is a false positive. A real problem with no alert is a false negative. A low-severity flaw the tool did report is still a true positive. A missed serious flaw is a false negative even if other hosts produced other alerts. Neither term is a public flaw identifier, and neither term is a severity score. The identifier names a flaw. The score, usually from 0 to 10, rates severity. These two terms rate the detector, not the flaw's name and not its score.\n",
        'r0': 'An alert that is not a real problem is the false-positive case. A real problem that produced no alert is the false-negative case. Host 10.4.2.20 was flagged because a banner still showed an old library version after the package was patched. The package manager showed the fix, so the alert was closed as a false positive. Host 10.4.2.40 had an authorization bug and no scanner plugin, so no alert fired. That silence was a false negative. When the detector reports a problem that is real, call that a true positive. A true negative is silence on a system that is fine. Do not describe either mistake as a severity score from 0 to 10. That score is a different field.\n',
        'r1': 'A false positive is an alert that is not a real problem. A false negative is a real problem with no alert. An old banner can cry wolf after a real fix. A missing check can stay quiet during a real bug. Confirm an alert before you call it an incident. Also test some systems that stayed quiet. Write the two mistakes in separate sentences. A real alert is not a false positive. A healthy quiet system is not a false negative. These words do not name a flaw. These words do not give a score from zero to ten. Track both mistakes or you will tune the wrong way.\n',
        'r2': 'A smoke alarm in a clinic hallway chirps on toast and stays silent during a real smolder in a closet. The toast chirp sends people running for nothing. The silent closet burns because nobody was called. Staff who yank the battery to stop the toast also stop the alarm that might have mattered at night. Staff who never walk the quiet halls assume silence means safety. A chart number on a patient wristband is not the chirp, and a thermometer reading is not the chirp. False positives are the toast alarms, alerts that are not a real problem. False negatives are the smolder with no alarm, a real problem and no alert.\n',
        'r4': 'Take one open alert and confirm whether a real problem exists. If the banner is stale and the package is patched, write false positive and name the banner. Pick one quiet host and test it another way. If you find a real bug with no alert, write false negative. Do not delete the noisy rule until you know what true hits it still catches. Do not swap the two labels in the weekly note.\n',
        'exam': 'A false positive is an alert that is not a real problem. A false negative is a real problem with no alert. Students swap those definitions. A reported low-severity flaw is still a true positive. A missed flaw is a false negative even when other alerts fired elsewhere. Neither label is a flaw identifier, and neither label is a score from 0 to 10. Closing an alert without a look can hide a real problem. Ignoring quiet hosts hides the misses.\n',
    },
    'Prioritizing vulnerabilities': {
        'body': "Prioritizing vulnerabilities is the decision about which findings get fixed first. Reachability, severity of effect, and the value of the asset drive the order. A long list sorted only by the day the row was opened, or only by one score, is not that decision. The next change window should go to the issue that can hurt the organization now. Everything else can have a later place, written down, rather than a silent drop to the bottom.\n\nThree questions sort a real queue. Can an attacker reach the flaw from the internet or from a sensitive network. What does success give them on this asset. How important is the process that asset serves. A standardized severity score, usually from 0 to 10, is a starting hint. It is not the decision. A public flaw identifier is only a name, so two tickets can mean the same bug. The digits inside the name are not a rank. A larger integer in the name is not a worse bug. Reachability can put a lower score ahead of a higher score that sits on a machine nobody can touch.\n\nMonday's queue held two items. The public website had a critical flaw on the customer login, reachable by anyone on the internet. A lab printer had a low finding and no path from outside the lab. The night window went to the website. The printer was placed on the monthly lab cycle. Ticket PRI-3 recorded the reason in one sentence: reachable and critical on a system that holds customer records, ahead of a low finding on an isolated printer. The website was not chosen because its flaw name contained a larger integer than the printer's name.\n\nWrite the order and the reason. Use reachability, impact, and asset value together. Let the score inform severity, then adjust for whether the flaw can be reached and what the system does. Do not let a huge export bully a small reachable set. Revisit the order when a new exploit appears or when a host changes exposure. A deferral needs a date and an owner. That dated acceptance is a separate record. Dropping a row because the list is tiring is not a priority decision.\n\nThe trap is to sort by the score alone, or by the digits in the flaw's public name, and call the sort a priority. The score starts the conversation. A high score on a system that is off the network can wait behind a medium score on the public website. The name of a flaw is not a severity at all. Priority is the order you will actually fix things, given what is reachable and what the exposed system is worth.\n",
        'r0': 'Order fixes by reachability, effect, and asset value, not by list length. On Monday the public website flaw was reachable on port 443 and sat in front of customer records. The lab printer finding was low and had no path from the internet. Ticket PRI-3 gave the night window to the website and the monthly lab cycle to the printer. A severity score, usually from 0 to 10, informed the critical and low labels and did not finish the choice. The public identifier on each row was only a name. A larger number inside that name would not have outranked a reachable login flaw. Write the reason next to the order.\n',
        'r1': 'Fix the problems that can hurt you first. A long list is not an order. Ask whether an attacker can reach the flaw. Ask what the system is worth. A severity number is only a start. That number usually runs from zero to ten. A public flaw name is not a rank. A bigger name is not a worse flaw. Put the internet-facing system ahead of a lab toy. Write the reason on the ticket. Do not sort only by the score. Do not sort by the digits in the name.\n',
        'r2': 'An emergency desk sees two people arrive together. One cannot breathe. The other has a small cut and is sitting up, talking, in a side chair far from the street door. The desk does not serve whoever drew the larger wristband number. The desk also does not treat a printed pain scale as the whole decision when one person is by the open door and the other is in a locked room. Staff write why the breathing patient went first. Prioritizing vulnerabilities is that desk for findings. Reachable harm on a valuable system goes ahead of a low problem on an isolated machine.\n',
        'r4': 'List the open findings you could touch in the next window. Mark which ones an outsider can reach and which assets they sit on. Write the order and one sentence of reason on the ticket. Do not sort by the digits inside a flaw name. Do not let a score alone move an isolated lab device ahead of a public system. Leave the actual install for the change record.\n',
        'exam': 'Priority is the fix order, based on reachability, severity, and asset value. A score, usually from 0 to 10, only starts that judgment. A high score on a system that is off the network can wait behind a lower score on the public website. A public flaw identifier is a name, not a severity, and a larger number in the name is not a higher rank. List length is not a priority. A deferral without a date is not a decision.\n',
    },
    'CVE identifiers': {
        'body': 'A CVE identifier is a public name for one known vulnerability. The letters are followed by a year and a number, as in CVE-2021-44228. The year is when the name was assigned. The number is a sequence so each flaw can be told apart from the others. Two analysts, a vendor, and a ticket system can cite that string and mean the same flaw. Nothing in the digits says how bad the flaw is. The identifier is not a severity.\n\nA numbering authority publishes the name with a short description once the flaw is public. Other databases then attach scores, affected versions, and references beside that name. The score people usually mean runs from 0 to 10 and lives in a different field. You can have a name with a low score or a name with a 10. You can have a serious bug in private code that has no public identifier at all, because nobody requested one. Looking up the exact string is how you avoid a nickname that covers several flaws. Sorting a backlog by the size of the number sorts noise.\n\nA ticket said only "Log4Shell on the billing host." That nickname is commonly tied to CVE-2021-44228, a remote code execution flaw in a Java logging library. A later related flaw received its own name, CVE-2021-45046, so the nickname alone could point at more than one bug. The engineer wrote CVE-2021-44228 on ticket VULN-88 and opened that exact record. The description matched the library in the build. The base score shown beside the record was 10.0. The engineer stored 10.0 in the score field and the identifier in the name field. A coworker who had ordered the queue by the numeric tail was stopped, because 44228 is not a rank and 10.0 is not part of the name.\n\nPut the full identifier on every ticket that refers to a published flaw. Look up that record, confirm the product and version, and only then talk about severity. Keep the score, if you use one, in the score field. If a scanner reports a nickname or a plugin number, map it to the identifier before two teams discuss it. If there is no public name, say so and keep your internal finding id. Do not invent a string. Do not treat the presence of a name as proof the flawed product is installed here. The inventory answers that question.\n\nThe trap is to treat the identifier as a severity or as the score. The name is not a severity. The score, usually from 0 to 10, is the separate rating. Saying the name "is a 10" mixes the two fields. A second trap is assuming a published name means the flaw is in your environment. The name lets you look the flaw up. Your asset list shows whether you run the affected product. A third trap is trusting a nickname when later flaws were given their own names.\n',
        'r0': 'Use the public name so two people mean one flaw, and do not read severity into the digits. CVE-2021-44228 names one remote code execution flaw in a Java logging library. The nickname Log4Shell is not a substitute, because CVE-2021-45046 later named a related flaw. Ticket VULN-88 stored the first identifier in the name field. The base score beside that record was 10.0, in the score field, on a scale that usually runs from 0 to 10. The integer 44228 is not a rank. A published name does not prove the library is in your build. Confirm the version, then discuss the score.\n',
        'r1': 'A CVE name is a shared label for one flaw. The year and the number are not a score. A high number is not a worse flaw. Severity lives in a different field. That score usually runs from zero to ten. Two people use the name so they mean the same bug. A nickname can point at the wrong bug. Write the full name on the ticket. Then look that exact name up. Do not sort work by the size of the number. A name does not mean the flaw is on your network.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic gives each patient a chart number so two nurses do not treat the wrong person under a similar nickname. The chart number is not the thermometer reading. A larger chart number does not mean a higher fever. The fever is written on a different line, and a person with no chart number can still be sick. Calling someone "the winter cough" can mix up two admissions that needed two charts. CVE identifiers are chart numbers for public flaws. The score, usually from zero to ten, is the thermometer line, and it is not printed inside the chart number.\n',
        'r4': 'Copy the full identifier onto the ticket, including the year and the number. Look up that exact record before you discuss impact. Write any score in a separate field from the name. Do not sort the queue by the size of the number. If the report uses only a nickname, map it to one identifier before you forward it. Do not invent a name when none exists.\n',
        'exam': 'A CVE identifier is a name, not a severity. The year and the sequence number do not rank the flaw. The score, usually from 0 to 10, is a separate field. CVE-2021-44228 can carry a base score of 10.0, and 10.0 is not part of the name. A nickname such as Log4Shell can blur later identifiers, including CVE-2021-45046. A published name does not mean the affected product is installed in your environment.\n',
    },
    'CVSS scores': {
        'body': 'CVSS scores are standardized severity numbers for a vulnerability, usually from zero to ten, derived from a vector of attack path, complexity, privileges, user interaction, scope, and effects on confidentiality, integrity, and availability. CVSS scores come in a base vector that ignores your environment, plus temporal and environmental adjustments when those are used. CVSS scores open the severity discussion, and local reach still decides whether that number waits or moves. CVSS scores may look severe on an unplugged lab host and still lose the week to a milder flaw on the internet-facing site.\n\nCVSS scores are not the CVE name; the name identifies the flaw and the score rates a severity. CVSS scores are not the full prioritization rubric, which also weighs asset value and active exploitation. CVSS scores should be cited with the version of the formula and the vector string, because a bare 9.8 cannot be audited. CVSS scores from a vendor and from a national database sometimes differ when the assumptions differ, and the ticket should say which vector was used.\n\nCVSS scores environmental metrics let you lower or raise the number when confidentiality requirements or modified attack paths differ locally. CVSS scores do not express exposure factor as a fraction of asset loss and do not express risk tolerance. CVSS scores of zero or near zero can still matter if the finding is a policy violation the rubric treats separately. CVSS scores should not be averaged across a host into a meaningless mean.\n\nCVSS scores communication to leadership works better as bands plus the top items than as a sea of decimals. CVSS scores evidence is the vector, the resulting number, and the source. CVSS scores after a mitigation may be recomputed with environmental metrics, with the original base left visible. CVSS scores training stops analysts from treating the color as the only sort key.\n\nCVSS scores are effective when two analysts given the same vector produce the same base number and then still write a local exposure note that can change the order. CVSS scores base vectors describe the worst reasonable case the publisher assumed, which may not be the configuration in this building. CVSS scores temporal values can fall when a fix exists and rise when exploit code is known, and those shifts should be dated. CVSS scores environmental modifications for a flaw that requires a local account can drop when that path is unavailable on the server.\n\nCVSS scores should be stored as the vector, not only the decimal, so a later formula change can be recomputed. CVSS scores disagreements between a vendor and a database are resolved by writing which one the rubric will use this quarter. CVSS scores of a flaw with a low confidentiality impact and a high availability impact matter more on a safety controller than on a brochure site. CVSS scores do not encode whether a control already blocks the attack path; the environmental or the local note does.\n\nCVSS scores in a dashboard can be grouped into bands for executives while analysts keep the vector. CVSS scores training should include one exercise where the same base number produces two different work orders because exposure differs. CVSS scores of chained bugs are not found by adding the decimals together. CVSS scores used as a contractual service level, such as a promise to fix above a band within a number of days, should match the tolerance statement.\n\nCVSS scores on a withdrawn issue should be removed from the active queue with the identifier. CVSS scores flavor, such as version three versus a newer revision, must be labeled because the numbers are not perfectly comparable. CVSS scores of a configuration finding may be absent, and the rubric still needs a way to rank that finding. CVSS scores are useful because they are shared language, and they are dangerous when they replace local judgment.\n\nCVSS scores in an audit sample should be reproducible by a second person from the stored vector. CVSS scores used in a contract should name the formula revision so a later revision does not silently rewrite the obligation. CVSS scores for a flaw that needs user interaction can be read differently on a kiosk than on an administrator workstation. CVSS scores stored without the vector should be treated as incomplete records.\n\nCVSS scores are a shared scale, and the local note is what makes the scale operational.',
        'r0': 'CVSS scores turn a vector of exploitability and impact into a severity number, typically zero through ten. CVSS scores include a base view and optional temporal and environmental adjustments. CVSS scores start the discussion and do not finish it, because local exposure can reorder work. CVSS scores are not CVE identifiers, which only name the flaw. CVSS scores should travel with the vector string and the formula version. CVSS scores must not be averaged into a single host grade. CVSS scores differ from exposure factor and from risk tolerance. CVSS scores proof is a reproducible vector plus a written local note. CVSS scores should be stored as the vector so they can be recomputed. CVSS scores from two publishers need a written choice when they disagree. CVSS scores of chained bugs are not a sum of decimals.',
        'r1': 'CVSS scores are standardized severity numbers. CVSS scores usually run from zero to ten. CVSS scores come from a vector string. CVSS scores have a base view. CVSS scores can add environmental adjustments. CVSS scores start the conversation. CVSS scores do not replace local exposure. CVSS scores are not CVE names. CVSS scores are not the full priority rubric. CVSS scores should not be averaged away. CVSS scores are not exposure factor. CVSS scores are not risk tolerance. CVSS scores should keep the vector string. CVSS scores from two sources need a written choice.',
        'r2': "CVSS scores are the avalanche number posted on a chalkboard at the trailhead. CVSS scores tell you the general danger the bulletin writers saw. CVSS scores do not walk your particular slope, which may be sheltered or may sit under a cornice. CVSS scores are not the catalog number of a single book and not the nurse's order of patients. CVSS scores of a high bulletin on a closed trail can wait while a moderate bulletin on the open ridge gets attention. CVSS scores should be copied with the symbols that produced them, not as a lonely digit. CVSS scores proof is the same number from the same symbols, plus a note about your own ridge.",
        'r4': "CVSS scores: record the vector and the formula version. CVSS scores: keep the base number visible. CVSS scores: add an environmental note when local exposure differs. CVSS scores: do not sort solely by the band. CVSS scores: do not average scores across a host. CVSS scores: say which publisher's vector you used when they differ. CVSS scores: separate the number from the CVE name. CVSS scores: separate the number from priority, exposure factor, and tolerance.",
        'exam': "CVSS scores items are severity numbers from a published vector, not the flaw's name. CVSS scores are not CVE identifiers and are not the whole prioritization decision. CVSS scores without the vector cannot be checked. CVSS scores that ignore a powered-off host will mis-order the week. CVSS scores are a start, and the environment finishes the argument.",
    },
    'Exposure factor': {
        'body': "Exposure factor is the fraction of an asset's value you expect to lose if one named event happens. The fraction runs from none of the value up to all of it. A partial loss may be written 0.40. A total loss of the value at stake is written 1. The fraction is not a dollar amount, and it is not how often the event is expected. Those are other inputs on the same worksheet. People who drop a severity score into this field are using the wrong scale.\n\nOne-event loss is the asset value multiplied by this fraction. You can write that as SLE = AV x EF. A file store valued at 80000 dollars, with an exposure factor of 0.40, produces a single loss of 32000 dollars. The 32000 is the expected loss for one occurrence. It is not the fraction. How often you expect the event is a separate rate. If that loss is expected 0.5 times a year, the annualized figure is 32000 times 0.5, which is 16000 dollars. Change only the rate, and the single-loss figure stays 32000. Change the fraction, and both figures move. Calling the rate an exposure factor is the mix-up this topic prevents.\n\nThe file store used 0.40 because a ransomware event was judged to take about forty percent of that 80000 dollar value: restoration work, some records not fully recovered, and notification. The same afternoon, an unencrypted laptop that held a complete copy of a dataset received an exposure factor of 1 for theft. A thief would have the whole set in the clear, so the fraction for that data is total. Ticket RISK-40 recorded both lines and did not copy 0.40 onto the laptop. The event does not eat the same share of every asset. An encrypted laptop, with the key not taken, would have forced a new fraction rather than a reused one.\n\nWrite the fraction next to the scenario and add a sentence that says why. Base it on what the event actually takes. When a control truly limits the loss, such as encryption that keeps a stolen disk unreadable, update the fraction and say so. Do not paste a score of 8 into the fraction field. A score usually runs from 0 to 10 and is not a portion of asset value. Do not write dollars in the fraction field. Dollars belong to asset value and to the single loss. Review the fraction when only a subset of the records remains on that system.\n\nStudents multiply the wrong pair. The fraction is the exposure factor inside SLE = AV x EF. The dollar result of one event is not the fraction. The yearly rate is not the fraction. A severity score is not the fraction. Business impact is the story of which process stops and who is hurt. Risk tolerance is how much of that risk leadership will carry. The fraction is none of those, even when all of them sit on one risk page.\n",
        'r0': 'Exposure factor is the fraction in SLE = AV x EF, the share of asset value lost if the event occurs once. A file store worth 80000 dollars at a factor of 0.40 has a single loss of 32000 dollars. Expected 0.5 times a year, the annualized loss is 16000 dollars. The 0.5 is the rate, not the fraction. Ticket RISK-40 gave an unencrypted laptop a factor of 1 for theft because the entire dataset would be in the clear. The laptop did not inherit 0.40 from the file store. A score from 0 to 10 does not belong in the fraction field. Dollars belong to value and to single loss, not to the fraction itself.\n',
        'r1': 'Exposure factor is the share you lose if an event hits. It is a fraction of the asset value. It is not a dollar figure. It is not how often the loss happens. Multiply value by the fraction for one loss. Eighty thousand times four tenths is thirty-two thousand. That result is one event, not a year. Half a time per year makes sixteen thousand yearly. A stolen cleartext laptop can mean the whole value. A score from zero to ten is not the fraction. Write the fraction and the reason together.\n',
        'r2': 'A storeroom owner draws a line on the wall to show how much of an aisle a flood would ruin. A line at the ceiling means the whole aisle goes. A line partway up means a portion, and the portion is not the same thing as the dollar tag on the shelf. How many floods arrive in a year is a mark on a calendar, not a higher line on the wall. A fever chart from the clinic next door does not belong on the plaster. Exposure factor is that line, the fraction of value one event would take. Asset value times the line is the loss for a single flood, and the calendar is a separate input.\n',
        'r4': "Name the asset and the event before you pick a fraction. Write the fraction as a portion of value, not as dollars and not as a yearly rate. If you compute one loss, multiply asset value by that fraction and label the product as one occurrence. Keep the yearly rate in a different field. Do not copy another asset's fraction onto a laptop without a reason. Do not paste a severity score into the fraction.\n",
        'exam': 'Exposure factor is the fraction of asset value lost if the event happens. SLE = AV x EF. Value 80000 and factor 0.40 give a single loss of 32000. A rate of 0.5 a year makes an annualized loss of 16000, and the rate is not the fraction. An unencrypted laptop can take a factor of 1 for the data on it. A score from 0 to 10 is not an exposure factor. Business impact and risk tolerance are different ideas on the same worksheet.\n',
    },
    'Environmental and business impact': {
        'body': 'Environmental and business impact ask what a flaw or an event does to this organization, not to a generic one. The questions are concrete. Which process stops. Which customer is hurt. Which legal duty is triggered. Which site can work around the failure, and for how long. Two copies of the same technical bug can have different answers. The impact lives in the context. It does not live in the bug\'s name, and it is not a fraction of a dollar value by itself.\n\nStart from the asset\'s role. A lobby kiosk that shows a floor map can fail without stopping pay. A payroll server that holds bank details can, with the same missing patch, delay wages and expose employee data under a privacy duty. You write the impact in those nouns: process, people, duty, and duration. A standardized score does not contain your payroll calendar or your contracts. A scoring system may also offer an environmental adjustment, and that adjustment is still a number. It does not name the duty or the customer. The exposure factor, when you are estimating loss, remains only the fraction of asset value. The fraction does not tell the story.\n\nThe same missing patch was found on the lobby kiosk and on the payroll server. On the kiosk, the technician noted that a failure blanks a public directory display and that the device holds a floor map. On payroll, the owner noted that a failure can delay pay for the whole staff and that the store holds bank details. The bug name matched. The impact lines did not. Ticket IMP-12 escalated payroll the same day and left the kiosk on the ordinary desktop cycle. The write-up used the process names. It did not paste a generic "high" with no noun attached, and it did not reuse the kiosk sentence on the payroll row.\n\nFor each serious finding, write the process affected, the data involved, and any duty to customers, staff, or regulators. Ask the owner to confirm that sentence before you brief anyone. Do not copy one impact line onto every host that shares a bug name. When a lab system is promoted into production, rewrite the impact before you reuse the old urgency. Leaders can act on a process name. They cannot act on a score that never says what breaks on Monday morning.\n\nStudents substitute the exposure factor for this story. The factor is the fraction of asset value inside the loss formula. Impact is what happens here. They also substitute risk tolerance. Tolerance is how much risk the organization agrees to carry, and for how long. A severe local impact can exist on a system leaders still refuse to leave exposed. That refusal is a tolerance choice made after the impact is understood. It is not a synonym for the impact. The kiosk and the payroll server are the proof that one bug is not one business result.\n',
        'r0': 'State the local harm: process, people, duty, and duration. The same missing patch hit a lobby kiosk and the payroll server. Ticket IMP-12 said the kiosk blanks a public floor map, while payroll can delay wages and exposes bank details under a privacy duty. The bug name was identical and the impact lines were not. A score, even one adjusted for a generic environment, stayed a number and did not replace those sentences. The exposure factor remained a fraction of asset value in a different field. Risk tolerance, the amount leadership will carry, was not filled in by copying the payroll sentence onto the kiosk.\n',
        'r1': 'This asks what the flaw does in this place. Which process stops. Which customer is hurt. Which duty applies. The same bug on two systems can differ. A lobby screen is not the payroll system. A fraction of value is a different idea. How much risk leaders accept is different too. Write the process and the data on the ticket. Do not paste one impact line onto every host. Ask the owner to confirm that sentence. A word like high, with no noun, is a weak line.\n',
        'r2': 'A leak in a clinic can hit the lobby fountain or the pharmacy refrigerator. Water on the marble annoys visitors and does not spoil medicine. Water in the refrigerator can ruin doses and trigger a reporting duty before the next clinic day. The pipe size can look the same on a drawing. The drawing does not name the insulin or the visitor map. A mark that says "four tenths of the stock" is a portion, not the story of who waits. Environmental and business impact is the local story of fountain versus refrigerator. The same flaw, like the same pipe, does not produce the same harm in both rooms.\n',
        'r4': 'Name the system and the process it serves before you write impact. Record which data is involved and which duty would apply if it leaked or the process stopped. Ask the owner to confirm that sentence. Do not copy the payroll sentence onto a lobby kiosk that shares only a bug name. Do not replace the sentence with a score or with a fraction of asset value. Leave the accept-or-fix decision for the tolerance record.\n',
        'exam': 'Impact is what the flaw does here: the process, the person, and the duty. The same bug on a lobby kiosk and on payroll is not the same business impact. Exposure factor is a fraction of asset value, not this story. Risk tolerance is how much of the risk leadership will carry. An environmental adjustment inside a score is still a number. A label of high, with no process noun, has not answered the question.\n',
    },
    'Risk tolerance': {
        'body': "Risk tolerance is how much risk the organization is willing to carry. It is a leadership boundary. It decides which findings can wait and which cannot. It is not the size of a loss, and it is not the fraction of an asset. Analysts estimate what would happen and how exposed the system is. Leaders state the line. A technician's personal comfort is not the organization's tolerance, and a busy manager's silence is not a decision.\n\nThe boundary is written so people can apply it without a fresh argument each time. It may say that a low finding on an isolated lab can remain open for thirty days if an owner is named, and that an internet-facing critical flaw on a production system cannot remain open overnight unless a fence is in place and an executive has accepted what remains. The statement does not calculate the exposure factor. It uses the impact and the exposure already described, then draws the line. Items under the line are scheduled inside the allowed window. Items over the line are fixed, fenced, or formally accepted by someone allowed to accept them.\n\nThe current statement gives low lab findings a thirty-day window with the lab owner on the ticket. It refuses to let an internet-facing critical flaw on a production site sit overnight without a fence and an executive acceptance. On Thursday a lab printer finding received a review date thirty days out, with the lab owner named. A critical flaw on the public enrollment site, found the same morning, was not given that window. Ticket TOL-4 scheduled a same-day change and cited the sentence being applied. The difference was the written line, not the lead's mood and not the fact that both rows existed.\n\nGet the line in writing from the people who may accept risk. Apply it the same way to two similar findings. When a finding exceeds it, escalate with the impact statement attached. Do not stretch a lab window to cover a public system because the patch is inconvenient. When leaders change the boundary, replace the old statement so analysts are not using a stale line. An expired window is not still allowed. Pair anything that must wait with a recorded decision, rather than with a quiet backlog.\n\nStudents define this boundary as the exposure factor or as the business impact. The factor is the fraction of asset value in the loss formula. Impact is what the event does to this organization. Tolerance is how much of that risk leadership will carry, and for how long. A high impact does not become acceptable because someone is tired. Acceptance inside the line is a written choice. An internet-facing critical flaw is outside the lab's thirty-day example unless leaders explicitly take it under a different recorded decision.\n",
        'r0': 'Risk tolerance is the written line for how much risk leadership will carry. Low findings on an isolated lab may stay open thirty days with an owner named. An internet-facing critical flaw on a production site may not sit overnight without a fence and an executive acceptance. On Thursday, ticket TOL-4 gave the lab printer the thirty-day date and scheduled the public enrollment flaw the same day. The exposure factor was not that line, and the impact story was not that line. Silence from a manager did not create a window. When the written line changes, replace the old text so two analysts do not apply two eras at once.\n',
        'r1': 'Risk tolerance is how much risk the organization will carry. Leaders set that line. Analysts describe the harm. They do not invent the line themselves. A low lab issue might wait thirty days. An internet-facing critical issue might not wait overnight. The difference is the written line. Silence from a busy manager is not a decision. Apply the same line to similar findings. Do not stretch a lab window onto a public site. A fraction of asset value is not this line. A story of business harm is not this line.\n',
        'r2': 'A head cook posts how much spoilage the kitchen will accept before service. A soft herb that wilts in a day can wait until the next delivery if the note says so. A warm tray of meat left out overnight cannot, and the note does not ask the newest cook to feel brave about it. The portion of a pantry shelf a spill would ruin is a different mark. The story of which ward misses a meal is a different mark again. Risk tolerance is the posted note about what the organization will carry. The thirty-day lab window and the overnight refusal are two lines on that note, applied as written.\n',
        'r4': 'Read the current tolerance statement before you set a due date. Write down which sentence you are applying to this finding. If the finding is inside the line, name the owner and the date. If it is outside the line, escalate instead of borrowing a lab window. Do not treat silence as acceptance. Do not edit the statement on the ticket to make a late patch look allowed.\n',
        'exam': "Risk tolerance is how much risk the organization will carry, set by leadership and written down. A thirty-day window for a low lab finding does not cover an internet-facing critical flaw overnight. Exposure factor is a fraction of asset value, not this line. Business impact is what the event does here, not this line. Silence is not acceptance. A technician's comfort is not the organization's boundary. An expired window is no longer the line.\n",
    },
    'Patch remediation': {
        'body': "Patch remediation puts the vendor's fix on the systems that need it, through the change process, with a way to go back. The vulnerable software is updated or replaced. A previous package or a snapshot is kept so a bad pilot can be undone. The work is not finished as a change when the file is merely downloaded. It is finished when the intended hosts are on the new build, except any host you deliberately rolled back and recorded.\n\nTest the package, pick a window, and send it to a pilot group first. Watch health. If the pilot stays well, move the rest. If the pilot breaks, roll that group back and do not continue. The change record names the build, the hosts, the window, and the rollback. In ordinary vulnerability work, that install is remediation of the vulnerable condition. During an incident the phase names are stricter. Isolating a host is containment. Deleting malware and patching the hole, after evidence is preserved, are eradication. The pilot ring is neither phase. It is a safety step inside the change so one bad package does not cross the fleet.\n\nChange CHG-778 addressed a flaw in the web tier. On Tuesday at 02:00, ten servers in the pilot ring received the package. Health checks stayed green through Wednesday. On Thursday the other forty servers received it. One host failed its health check and was returned to the previous package, which the change had kept in the repository. The ticket listed fifty hosts intended, forty-nine on the new build, and one reverted. The installer that had sat in a shared folder the week before was not remediation. Remediation began when the pilot actually ran, and it was not uniform until the revert was written down.\n\nUse a change record, a pilot, and a rollback you have actually kept. Do not patch the whole estate in one motion when a pilot ring exists. After the rollout, verify with a rescan or a version check. That proof is its own step and is not the install. If you cannot patch yet, do not describe a calendar hold as a fix. A fence that leaves the old software in place is a compensating control. A dated decision to live with the finding is an exception. Keep the old package until the new build has stayed healthy for the period your change rule requires.\n\nStudents call a firewall rule a patch. The rule may limit who can connect while the old software remains, and that is not the vendor fix. They call the pilot containment. Containment, in an incident, is isolating a host. They call a downloaded file remediation. The file in a folder has not changed a single server. They close the change before any later observation shows the flaw gone. The install, the fence, the dated wait, and the later proof are four different sentences, and the ticket should keep them apart.\n",
        'r0': 'Apply the fix through a change, with a pilot and a rollback. CHG-778 patched ten web servers at 02:00 on Tuesday. Health stayed green through Wednesday, and forty more servers followed on Thursday. One host was returned to the previous package kept in the repository. The record showed forty-nine hosts on the new build and one revert. The installer in a shared folder the week before had changed nothing. Cutting a host off from the network is the containment phase. Once evidence is preserved, removing the malware and closing the hole with a patch count as eradication. The pilot itself is a change safety step, not containment. A firewall fence is not this install.\n',
        'r1': 'Put the vendor fix on the systems that need it. Use the change process. Keep a way to go back. Try a small group first. Watch that group before you continue. Roll that group back if it gets sick. Downloading the file is not the fix. A fence while you wait is not the fix. A dated decision to wait is not the fix. A later check proves the result. The small group is a safety step. It is not a quarantine of an attack.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic engineer replaces a failing valve and keeps the old valve on the bench. A single sink is tried first. If that sink runs clear through the next day, the other sinks get the same part. If the pilot sink leaks, the old valve goes back on and the rest of the floor waits. A rope around a broken sink, while the bad valve stays in place, is not a new valve. A note that says the repair can wait until spring is not a new valve either. Patch remediation is the valve replacement, pilot and rollback included. The rope and the spring note are different decisions, even when they share the same work order.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the change and list the pilot hosts, the window, and the rollback package. After the pilot, write down whether health stayed acceptable. Do not send the package to the rest of the fleet if the pilot failed. Record any host you reverted. Do not call a downloaded file a completed fix. Do not call a firewall fence the same step as the install.\n',
        'exam': 'Remediation means the fix is applied, with a way back. A firewall fence while the old software remains is a compensating control. A dated decision to wait is an exception. A later scan that no longer shows the flaw is verification. Isolating a host is containment. Deleting malware and patching the hole, after evidence is preserved, are eradication. The pilot is a change safety step, not containment. A downloaded installer that no host has run is not remediation.\n',
    },
    'Compensating controls for vulnerabilities': {
        'body': 'A compensating control reduces risk when you cannot put the patch on yet. The vulnerable software is still present. The control changes who can reach it, what it is allowed to do, or how closely a real restraint limits abuse, so a successful attack is harder or narrower. It is a fence, not a cure. You use it in the gap before a fix, or on a device the vendor will not let you patch. Calling the fence a patch teaches the next person that the flaw is gone when the scan will still see it.\n\nThe fence has to match the way the flaw is abused. A tight access list, so only one management host can connect, is useful when the danger is reaching a service. A rule that blocks one dangerous request pattern can help when the path is a specific message. Removing the service from networks where it does not belong shrinks the audience. An access list that still allows the whole company to reach the vulnerable service has not compensated much. Write what is blocked and what is still allowed. Recheck on a date, because fences drift when someone adds a convenience rule. Any public name for the flaw stays open. A score does not become zero because a router rule exists.\n\nA medical analyzer could not take the operating-system patch. The vendor would withdraw support if the image changed. The network team allowed only management host 10.8.1.4 to reach the analyzer, and the analyzer was not permitted to open outbound connections. A test from a second admin laptop failed to connect. The vulnerable software was still installed. Ticket FENCE-2 kept the finding open, named the access list, named the single allowed host, and recorded the failed test. Nobody wrote patched on the row.\n\nChoose a control you can test, then test it from a host that should be denied. Record the deny. Set a review date, and keep the real patch on the plan for the day the vendor allows it. When that day comes, install the fix and retire the story that the fence was enough. Do not stack the words "we are watching it" in place of a restraint you can fail on purpose. Watching can help you notice abuse. It does not block the connection the access list blocks. Pair the fence with a written decision to wait if policy requires one. The decision and the fence are still two objects.\n\nStudents mark the finding remediated when only the fence is in place. Remediation means the fix is applied. The fence leaves the flaw in the product. An exception is the written, time-boxed decision to live with that state. You often need the fence and the exception together. Neither one is a rescan that shows the flaw gone. The scan of the analyzer should still report the old software, and that report is expected until the software changes. Treating the expected report as a failed fence, or treating the fence as a finished patch, both misread the ticket.\n',
        'r0': 'A compensating control lowers exposure while the vulnerable software remains. The medical analyzer could not be patched without losing vendor support. Only host 10.8.1.4 was allowed to reach it, and outbound connections from the analyzer were denied. A second admin laptop failed its connection test. Ticket FENCE-2 left the finding open and recorded the access list plus the failed test. The row was not marked patched. A severity score does not fall to zero because the list exists. A written decision to wait is a separate record. When a real fix becomes allowed, apply it and do not leave the fence as the permanent story.\n',
        'r1': 'Use a fence when you cannot patch yet. The flawed software is still there. The fence shrinks who can reach it. One management machine might be the only path. Test from a machine that should be denied. Write down what is still allowed. The finding stays open. Do not mark the row as patched. A written wait is a separate decision. A new scan may still see the flaw. That result can be expected. Set a date to look again.\n',
        'r2': 'A warehouse cannot repair a cutter this month, so a temporary rail goes up and only one trained operator may step inside. The cutter is still dull in the dangerous way. The rail does not sharpen it. A visitor who should stay out is asked to try the gate, and a refused entry is the proof the rail is doing something. A note that says the repair can wait is not the rail. A sharp replacement blade is not the rail either. Compensating control is that rail around a flaw you cannot yet remove. The finding stays open because the cutter, like the unpatched software, is still there.\n',
        'r4': 'Name the flaw you cannot patch yet and the restraint you added. Record which host is still allowed to connect and which test host was denied. Leave the finding open. Do not write patched on the row. Set a date to retest the restraint. Do not remove the real fix from the plan. Keep any written decision to wait in a separate note.\n',
        'exam': 'A compensating control reduces risk while the vulnerable software remains. It is not remediation, and the finding stays open. An access list that allows only one management host is a fence, not a patch. A scan that still reports the flaw can be expected. An exception is the separate, time-boxed decision to live with that state. Watching without a tested restraint does not replace the fence. A score does not become zero because a router rule exists.\n',
    },
    'Exceptions and exemptions': {
        'body': 'An exception is a recorded, time-boxed decision to live with a finding. An exemption is a formal waiver of a requirement, granted by someone with authority to set that requirement aside. Silence is neither. Both are written, both name a decision maker, and neither one changes the software by itself. The exception admits a gap will remain until a date. The exemption is the formal waiver. A hallway remark, a backlog comment that says later, and an unread mail are not records. The scanner will keep reporting the flaw either way.\n\nThe exception record names the finding, the asset, the owner, the reason, the end date, and the compensating control if one is required. When the date arrives, the exception expires. Someone renews it with a new decision, or the finding returns to the fix queue. An exemption names the requirement, the asset, and the approver, and it states that the requirement is waived rather than merely delayed. Scope matters. A waiver for one instrument does not waive the same control on the payroll server. The open scan row can remain. The record explains why that row is allowed. It does not delete the row, and it does not pretend the vendor fix was installed.\n\nLegacy billing could not take a library update until the vendor shipped a build. Record EX-55 named owner Dana Cho, was signed 16 October 2026, and ended on 14 January 2027, a ninety-day window. It cited the network fence already in place as the compensating control, and the approver signed it. The finding stayed visible on the scan. A different analyst who said "we will get to it" in a meeting produced no record, no date, and no owner. That remark was not an exception and not an exemption. When 14 January 2027 arrives, EX-55 is renewed in writing or the patch becomes mandatory again. The calendar ends the decision, not memory.\n\nRefuse to treat silence as acceptance. Require an owner, a scope, a reason, and an approver. Require an end date on an exception. Attach the fence that makes the wait defensible, and keep that fence described as a fence. Review the item before it expires. When it expires, close it with a fix or write a new decision. Do not let a stack of expired exceptions become the real policy. Do not relabel a delay as an exemption to escape the date. An exemption still needs authority, scope, and a written waiver. It is not a softer word for "later."\n\nStudents use the two words as synonyms, and they use both for a vague delay. An exception is time-boxed acceptance of a finding. An exemption is a formal waiver. Neither is a patch, and neither is proof the flaw is gone. An expired exception is not still valid. A compensating control can sit beside an exception, but the control is the fence and the exception is the decision to wait. The sentence "we will get to it" creates neither document. A public flaw name on the row does not expire the decision or extend it.\n',
        'r0': 'An exception is a time-boxed, written acceptance of a finding. An exemption is a formal waiver by someone allowed to set the requirement aside. EX-55 named Dana Cho, started 16 October 2026, and ended 14 January 2027, ninety days later. It cited the network fence as the compensating control and kept the scan row open. A meeting remark, "we will get to it," had no owner and no date, so it was neither record. On 14 January 2027 the exception expires unless a new decision is written. Silence does not renew it. A waiver for one legacy application does not cover payroll. The fence remains a fence, not a patch.\n',
        'r1': 'An exception is a written decision to live with a finding. It has an end date. An exemption is a formal waiver. Silence is neither one. Name an owner on the record. Name the reason and the approver. A fence can sit beside the decision. The fence is not the decision. A hallway promise is not a record. An expired date puts the finding back in line. Do not call a delay an exemption. The scanner can still show the flaw.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic door can be left on a latch only with a permission slip that names a person and a last day. When that day passes, the latch is not still approved. A formal waiver is a different paper: the director sets the usual rule aside for one room, in writing, on purpose. A nurse saying "we will get to the lock" in the hallway produces neither paper. A rope across the door can be there at the same time, and the rope is not the slip. Exceptions and exemptions are those two papers for a finding you are not fixing today. The end date is what stops a temporary yes from becoming the permanent rule.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the record and confirm it names an owner, a scope, a reason, and an approver. If it is an exception, write down the end date and check that the date has not passed. Note any fence separately from the decision. Do not accept a spoken "later" as either document. Do not mark the flaw fixed because the record exists. Do not extend the date by editing it quietly after it lapses.\n',
        'exam': 'An exception is a recorded, time-boxed decision to live with a finding. An exemption is a formal waiver. Silence, including "we will get to it," is neither. An expired exception is not still valid. A compensating control can accompany an exception and is not the exception. Neither document applies the patch or proves the flaw is gone. A waiver for one asset does not cover a different asset. The scan may still show the finding while the record is in force.\n',
    },
    'Rescanning and verification': {
        'body': 'Rescanning and verification prove a claim about a finding. Either the finding is gone, or the compensating control you named is actually in place. A wish that the patch worked is not verification. A change ticket moved to done is not verification. The proof is a new observation. The same scan no longer reports the flaw, a version command shows the fixed build, or a connection test shows the fence holds. The observation, with a time, is what the ticket needs.\n\nFor a patch, run the same scanner profile, or an equivalent check, against the same host after the change. Compare the old finding with the new output. Absent means this check no longer sees it. Still present means the service may not have restarted, the wrong host was patched, or the package was not the fix for that flaw. For a compensating control, a rescan may still show the vulnerable software, and that does not by itself mean the fence failed. Verification then tests the control. A host that should be blocked cannot connect, and the one allowed host can. Write the observation and the time.\n\nHost web-12 was patched on Thursday for one finding. On Friday the same scan profile ran against web-12. The finding from Monday was absent. The analyst pasted the clean result and the scan time into the ticket and closed it. Host web-13 had been marked patched in the change tool, but Friday\'s scan still reported the flaw. The service on web-13 had never been restarted, so the old code was still in memory. That ticket stayed open. For the unpatchable analyzer, the scan still reported the old software, which was expected. Verification was a test from a second laptop, which failed to connect, plus a note that only the management host succeeded. That deny, not a claim of patched, was the proof filed on the fence.\n\nRescan after you patch, using a method that would have seen the original finding. If it remains, do not close. If you rely on a fence, test the fence from the deny side and attach the output. Do not close fifty hosts because one of them looked clean. Do not confuse this proof with a monthly note to leadership. The note reports status. Verification creates the evidence the note should cite. A false positive, an alert that was never a real problem, is a different close reason. You are not verifying a fix when the banner was stale and the package was already patched.\n\nStudents close the ticket when the change is approved or when the package is installed, before any new observation. That is not verification. They expect a compensating control to make the vulnerability scan go silent. The software can remain vulnerable, and the scan can keep saying so. The proof for a fence is a test of the fence. They also call a false positive "verified fixed." A false positive was not a real problem. Verification of a fix shows a real finding has gone, or a real control holds, and the ticket should say which of those two observations was made.\n',
        'r0': "Prove the finding is gone, or prove the fence holds, with a new observation. Web-12 was patched on Thursday. Friday's scan, using the same profile, no longer reported Monday's finding, and the ticket stored that result and the scan time. Web-13 was marked done in the change tool, yet Friday's scan still reported the flaw because the service never restarted. That ticket stayed open. The analyzer's scan still showed the old software. Verification there was a failed connection from a second laptop, with only the management host allowed. A monthly leadership note is not this proof. An alert that was never a real problem is not a verified fix.\n",
        'r1': 'Check again after you claim a fix. Run the same kind of scan. A missing finding is the proof you want. A change marked done is not proof. If the service never restarted, the old bug can remain. Write the scan time on the ticket. A fence is proved by a connection test. The scan may still show the old software. That can be fine if the fence holds. Test from a machine that should fail. Do not close a whole group from one success. Hope is not a result you can file.\n',
        'r2': 'A nurse changes a dressing and then looks at the wound again before anyone writes healed on the chart. The slip that says the gauze was fetched is not the look. If the old dressing is still on the patient because the work stopped halfway, the chart stays open. A rail around the bed is checked by trying to step past it from the wrong side, and the wound can still be there while the rail holds. Counting the rails in a monthly meeting does not replace the step. Rescanning and verification are that second look. The clean result, or the refused step from the wrong side, is what belongs on the ticket.\n',
        'r4': 'After the change, run the same check that first reported the finding. Paste the new result and the time into the ticket. If the finding is still present, leave the ticket open and note whether the service was restarted. For a fence, test a connection that should fail and record the deny. Do not close every host because one host looked clean. Do not call a leadership summary the verification.\n',
        'exam': 'Verification is a new observation, not a change ticket marked done. After a patch, the same scan should no longer report the flaw, and the ticket should record that result. A service that never restarted can keep the old flaw in memory. A compensating control may leave the scan positive. Prove the fence with a deny test. A false positive was never a real problem and is not a verified fix. A monthly status note reports the result. It does not create it.\n',
    },
    'Vulnerability reporting': {
        'body': 'Vulnerability reporting explains to each audience which issues remain, which fixes landed, which accepts are still in force, and which dates have slipped, using words that audience can act on. Vulnerability reporting to leadership is a short list of open high items with owners and dates, not a raw scan dump. Vulnerability reporting to system owners is specific enough to act, with the finding, the asset, and the expected decision. Vulnerability reporting cadence should be steady so a quiet month is visible rather than skipped.\n\nVulnerability reporting includes exceptions and their end dates, or accepted risk hides. Vulnerability reporting includes verification status, or fixes are claimed without evidence. Vulnerability reporting is not the patch, the fence, the waiver text, or the rescan technique; it is how those facts are communicated. Vulnerability reporting metrics can show aging of the top tier and coverage of the scan, and they should not celebrate a drop that came from muting checks.\n\nVulnerability reporting audiences differ, and a regulator packet is not the same note as an internal stand-up. Vulnerability reporting must protect sensitive exploit detail while still being honest about impact. Vulnerability reporting evidence is the dated note, the audience, and the decisions it asked for. Vulnerability reporting after an incident should match what the incident review later finds, or trust collapses.\n\nVulnerability reporting ownership is a person who can explain the numbers without hiding late items. Vulnerability reporting is effective when a reader can name the next action, the owner, and the date without opening the scanner. Vulnerability reporting calendars should survive vacations, with a deputy author named. Vulnerability reporting of a bad month should still go out, because silence reads as concealment.\n\nVulnerability reporting charts can show age bands for the top tier and a separate line for scan coverage. Vulnerability reporting to a board should avoid exploit detail and should include decisions requested, such as an acceptance above the usual line. Vulnerability reporting to system owners can be a portal view filtered to their assets, refreshed more often than the board note. Vulnerability reporting definitions of fixed, accepted, and open should be written once and reused so two months can be compared.\n\nVulnerability reporting of muted checks should appear as a coverage warning, not as an improvement in the fix rate. Vulnerability reporting after a major incident should align with the incident timeline so the two stories do not contradict. Vulnerability reporting drafts should be reviewed by someone who can spot a missing late item. Vulnerability reporting archives let an auditor see what leadership was told in a given month.\n\nVulnerability reporting of exceptions includes the end date in the same table as the open fixes. Vulnerability reporting tone should be factual, with owners and dates, rather than a pile of alarming adjectives.',
        'r0': 'Vulnerability reporting translates the program into audience-specific updates: open items, fixes, acceptances, and lateness. Vulnerability reporting for executives stays short and names owners and dates. Vulnerability reporting for operators stays actionable. Vulnerability reporting includes exception end dates and whether verification happened. Vulnerability reporting does not treat a muted scanner as progress. Vulnerability reporting is the communication, not the patch, the fence, the waiver, or the rescan method. Vulnerability reporting proof is a dated note that forces a decision a reader can point at. Vulnerability reporting should still go out in a bad month. Vulnerability reporting definitions of open, fixed, and accepted must stay stable across months. Vulnerability reporting of muted checks belongs in a coverage warning. Vulnerability reporting archives show what leadership was told.',
        'r1': 'Vulnerability reporting informs the right audience. Vulnerability reporting lists open items, fixes, and accepts. Vulnerability reporting includes owners and dates. Vulnerability reporting stays short for leadership. Vulnerability reporting stays actionable for operators. Vulnerability reporting includes exception end dates. Vulnerability reporting includes verification status. Vulnerability reporting does not dump raw scan noise upward. Vulnerability reporting does not treat muted checks as success. Vulnerability reporting is not the patch itself. Vulnerability reporting is not the fence itself. Vulnerability reporting is not the waiver itself.',
        'r2': 'Vulnerability reporting is the lighthouse log read to the harbor master at a set hour. Vulnerability reporting says which lamps failed, who is climbing the tower, and which lamp the council agreed to leave dark until spring. Vulnerability reporting does not hand the master every tide table ever printed. Vulnerability reporting is not the climb, not the rope at the base, not the council permit, and not a second look through the lens. Vulnerability reporting of a dark lamp hidden by silence is a failed reading. Vulnerability reporting proof is a master who can point to the next climb and the date without visiting the lens room.',
        'r4': 'Vulnerability reporting: send a steady cadence. Vulnerability reporting: list top open items with owners and dates. Vulnerability reporting: include accepts and their end dates. Vulnerability reporting: include what verification showed. Vulnerability reporting: keep executive notes short. Vulnerability reporting: keep owner notes actionable. Vulnerability reporting: do not count muted checks as improvement. Vulnerability reporting: leave exploit recipes out of wide audiences.',
        'exam': 'Vulnerability reporting items are audience-ready status, not the repair itself. Vulnerability reporting is not patch remediation, compensating controls, exceptions, or rescanning, though it cites them. Vulnerability reporting that pastes raw scanner noise at executives fails the purpose. Vulnerability reporting that hides late items or muted rules is misleading. Vulnerability reporting succeeds when the reader knows the next owner and date.',
    },
}
